const TOPICS = ["SELECT and DISTINCT", "Filtering with WHERE", "Sorting and limiting", "Aggregation and COUNT DISTINCT", "GROUP BY", "HAVING", "CASE expressions", "Joins", "Subqueries", "CTEs", "Window basics", "Ranking and deduplication", "LAG LEAD and period change", "NTILE and quantile segmentation", "Date and time logic", "Cohort retention", "Funnel conversion and rate math", "Cross-product attachment", "Subtotals with ROLLUP", "Causal setup treatment and control"];
const LESSONS = {"SELECT and DISTINCT": "\n<p><b>SELECT</b> is how you ask for columns from a table. <code>SELECT * FROM customers</code> returns every column; naming columns like <code>SELECT first_name, country FROM customers</code> returns just those, in that order.</p>\n<p><b>DISTINCT</b> removes duplicate rows from the result, so <code>SELECT DISTINCT country FROM customers</code> gives one row per country. It applies to the whole row of selected columns, not a single column.</p>\n<p>In the consumer growth role this is the constant first move: pull the right columns from customers, transactions, or events before you shape anything.</p>\n", "Filtering with WHERE": "\n<p><b>WHERE</b> keeps only rows that match a condition. Combine conditions with <code>AND</code> and <code>OR</code>. Useful operators: <code>=</code>, <code>&lt;&gt;</code> (not equal), <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>BETWEEN a AND b</code>, <code>IN (...)</code>, <code>LIKE 'A%'</code> for text patterns, and <code>IS NULL</code> / <code>IS NOT NULL</code>.</p>\n<p>Text comparisons are case sensitive here, and dates are stored as text in <code>YYYY-MM-DD</code> form, which sorts and compares correctly as text.</p>\n<p>This is how you scope an analysis to a segment, a country, a date range, or a transaction status.</p>\n", "Sorting and limiting": "\n<p><b>ORDER BY</b> sorts the result. Default is ascending; add <code>DESC</code> for descending. You can sort by several columns: <code>ORDER BY amount DESC, transaction_id</code>.</p>\n<p><b>LIMIT n</b> returns only the first n rows after sorting, and <b>OFFSET k</b> skips the first k. So <code>ORDER BY amount DESC LIMIT 5 OFFSET 5</code> returns the 6th through 10th largest.</p>\n<p>Top-N and bottom-N ranking of customers, products, or markets is a daily task when sizing opportunities.</p>\n", "Aggregation and COUNT DISTINCT": "\n<p>Aggregate functions collapse many rows into one value: <code>COUNT(*)</code>, <code>SUM()</code>, <code>AVG()</code>, <code>MIN()</code>, <code>MAX()</code>.</p>\n<p><b>COUNT(DISTINCT col)</b> counts unique values, which is how you get active customers, unique products, or distinct markets rather than raw row counts.</p>\n<p>Wrap results in <code>ROUND(value, 2)</code> to control decimals. Aggregates power every metric you report: revenue, active users, average order value.</p>\n", "GROUP BY": "\n<p><b>GROUP BY</b> splits rows into groups and runs the aggregate once per group. <code>SELECT country, COUNT(*) FROM customers GROUP BY country</code> gives one row per country.</p>\n<p>List several columns to group across dimensions at once: <code>GROUP BY country, segment</code> produces one row per country and segment combination. This multi-dimensional slicing (product by segment by geography) is the backbone of portfolio analysis.</p>\n<p>Every non-aggregated column in the SELECT must appear in the GROUP BY.</p>\n", "HAVING": "\n<p><b>HAVING</b> filters groups after aggregation, the way <code>WHERE</code> filters rows before it. Use it to keep groups that pass an aggregate test.</p>\n<p><code>GROUP BY customer_id HAVING SUM(amount) &gt; 500</code> keeps only high-value customers. You cannot put <code>SUM(amount) &gt; 500</code> in a WHERE clause because the sum does not exist until after grouping.</p>\n<p>This isolates cohorts worth acting on: heavy spenders, high-frequency users, popular products.</p>\n", "CASE expressions": "\n<p><b>CASE</b> is inline if-then logic. <code>CASE WHEN amount &gt;= 200 THEN 'high' ELSE 'low' END</code> returns a value per row.</p>\n<p>Its most powerful use is <b>conditional aggregation</b>: <code>SUM(CASE WHEN status='completed' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)</code> counts only completed rows inside a wider aggregate. This lets you compute several segmented metrics in a single pass.</p>\n<p>You will reach for this constantly to build funnel counts and labelled buckets.</p>\n", "Joins": {"INNER JOIN": "<p><b>INNER JOIN</b><br>An INNER JOIN combines rows from two tables where the join condition matches on both sides.<br>If a row on either side has no match, it is dropped from the result entirely.<br>It is the default join type, and the one to reach for when you only want records that exist in both tables.</p><p><b>Example question:</b> Bring in each transaction's readable product name next to its amount.</p><div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>SELECT t.transaction_id, p.product_name, t.amount\nFROM transactions t\nJOIN products p ON t.product_id = p.product_id\nWHERE t.transaction_id = 1;</pre></div><p>Result: transaction_id 1, product_name 'Xoom', amount 38.05. If product_id 5 didn't exist in products, this row would simply be dropped &mdash; that's the defining behavior of INNER JOIN.</p><p><b>How to decide which join to use</b><br>Start by asking what you actually need in the result.<br>If you only care about rows that have a match on both sides, and you're fine dropping anything that doesn't, that's an INNER JOIN.<br>If you need to keep every row from one side no matter what &mdash; even rows with nothing to match &mdash; that's a LEFT JOIN, and the table you want to keep in full goes on the left.<br>If you're comparing rows in a table to other rows in that very same table, rather than combining two different tables, that's a SELF JOIN &mdash; it still behaves like an INNER or LEFT JOIN underneath, you're just aliasing the same table twice so SQL can tell the two \"copies\" apart.<br>A simple habit for beginners: write the query as an INNER JOIN first and run it. If rows you expected are missing, that's your signal to switch that join to a LEFT JOIN.</p>", "LEFT JOIN": "<p><b>LEFT JOIN</b><br>A LEFT JOIN keeps every row from the left table, whether or not it finds a match on the right.<br>Unmatched right-side columns come back as NULL instead of the row being dropped.<br>This is the standard way to answer \"who or what is missing\" questions, like customers who never transacted.</p><p><b>Example question:</b> Find customers who signed up but never made a transaction.</p><div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>SELECT c.customer_id, c.first_name\nFROM customers c\nLEFT JOIN transactions t ON c.customer_id = t.customer_id\nWHERE t.transaction_id IS NULL;</pre></div><p>Result: 25 customers, including customer_id 4 (Sami) and 6 (Quinn). Their transaction-side columns come back NULL, and the WHERE clause keeps only those NULL rows &mdash; the rows an INNER JOIN would have silently dropped.</p><p><b>How to decide which join to use</b><br>Start by asking what you actually need in the result.<br>If you only care about rows that have a match on both sides, and you're fine dropping anything that doesn't, that's an INNER JOIN.<br>If you need to keep every row from one side no matter what &mdash; even rows with nothing to match &mdash; that's a LEFT JOIN, and the table you want to keep in full goes on the left.<br>If you're comparing rows in a table to other rows in that very same table, rather than combining two different tables, that's a SELF JOIN &mdash; it still behaves like an INNER or LEFT JOIN underneath, you're just aliasing the same table twice so SQL can tell the two \"copies\" apart.<br>A simple habit for beginners: write the query as an INNER JOIN first and run it. If rows you expected are missing, that's your signal to switch that join to a LEFT JOIN.</p>", "SELF JOIN": "<p><b>SELF JOIN</b><br>A self join joins a table to itself, using two aliases to tell the two \"copies\" apart.<br>It's how you compare rows within the same table to each other, like pairing customers who share an attribute.<br>The join condition typically excludes matching a row with itself, often with something like a.id &lt; b.id to also avoid duplicate reversed pairs.</p><p><b>Example question:</b> Pair up customers who share the same country, for a regional referral campaign.</p><div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>SELECT a.first_name AS customer_a, b.first_name AS customer_b, a.country\nFROM customers a\nJOIN customers b ON a.country = b.country AND a.customer_id &lt; b.customer_id\nORDER BY a.country, a.customer_id, b.customer_id\nLIMIT 5;</pre></div><p>Result: 280 pairs in total; the first is (Sami, Yusuf, France). The <code>a.customer_id &lt; b.customer_id</code> condition is what stops 'Sami paired with Yusuf' and 'Yusuf paired with Sami' both showing up as separate rows.</p><p><b>How to decide which join to use</b><br>Start by asking what you actually need in the result.<br>If you only care about rows that have a match on both sides, and you're fine dropping anything that doesn't, that's an INNER JOIN.<br>If you need to keep every row from one side no matter what &mdash; even rows with nothing to match &mdash; that's a LEFT JOIN, and the table you want to keep in full goes on the left.<br>If you're comparing rows in a table to other rows in that very same table, rather than combining two different tables, that's a SELF JOIN &mdash; it still behaves like an INNER or LEFT JOIN underneath, you're just aliasing the same table twice so SQL can tell the two \"copies\" apart.<br>A simple habit for beginners: write the query as an INNER JOIN first and run it. If rows you expected are missing, that's your signal to switch that join to a LEFT JOIN.</p>"}, "Subqueries": "<p><b>Subquery</b><br>A subquery is a query nested inside another.<br>A <b>scalar subquery</b> returns exactly one value, useful in a WHERE clause: <code>WHERE amount &gt; (SELECT AVG(amount) FROM transactions)</code>.<br><code>IN (SELECT ...)</code> tests whether a value appears in a list produced by another query.<br><code>EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM ... WHERE ...)</code> tests whether any matching row exists at all.<br>A subquery that references a column from the outer query is <b>correlated</b> &mdash; it re-runs once per outer row, which is slower but necessary for row-specific comparisons.<br>One that doesn't reference the outer query is non-correlated and only runs once.</p><p class=\"note\"><b>Note</b><br><code>NOT IN (SELECT ...)</code> silently returns zero rows if that subquery's result contains even a single NULL.<br>Prefer <code>NOT EXISTS</code> for anti-join style checks to avoid this trap.</p><p><b>Worked example:</b> to flag transactions above the overall average, compare each row to a scalar subquery:</p><div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>SELECT transaction_id, amount\nFROM transactions\nWHERE amount &gt; (SELECT AVG(amount) FROM transactions)\nORDER BY transaction_id;</pre></div><p>The subquery evaluates once to 208.21.<br>transaction_id 1 at 38.05 is filtered out for being below that average, while transaction_id 4 at 218.5 is the first row that qualifies.</p><p>Subqueries express comparisons against a benchmark, which is common when flagging above-average or qualifying customers.</p>", "CTEs": "<p><b>Common Table Expression</b><br>A CTE names a subquery up front with <code>WITH name AS (...)</code>, then lets you reference it like a table.<br>It makes multi-step logic readable.<br>You can chain several: <code>WITH a AS (...), b AS (SELECT ... FROM a) SELECT ... FROM b</code>.<br>Each step builds on the previous, which is exactly how you would narrate a segmentation or funnel build in an interview.</p><p class=\"note\"><b>Note</b><br>A CTE is not a temporary table &mdash; in SQLite (and most engines by default) it's just a named subquery, inlined and re-evaluated wherever it's referenced.<br>It only exists for the duration of the single statement it's attached to, so it can't be reused across separate queries the way a view or table can.</p><p><b>Worked example:</b> name the per-customer total up front, then filter it like a table:</p><div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>WITH spend AS (\n  SELECT customer_id, SUM(amount) AS total\n  FROM transactions\n  GROUP BY customer_id\n)\nSELECT customer_id, total\nFROM spend\nWHERE customer_id = 1;</pre></div><p>Customer 1's four transactions of 38.05, 181.54, 89.52 and 218.5 sum to 527.61, which the outer query can then filter or select from spend by name instead of repeating the aggregation.</p><p>Prefer CTEs over deeply nested subqueries when the logic has stages.</p>", "Window basics": "<p><b>Window function</b><br>A window function computes across a set of rows related to the current row, without collapsing them like GROUP BY does.<br>The <code>OVER (...)</code> clause defines that window.<br><code>SUM(amount) OVER (ORDER BY txn_date)</code> gives a running total.<br><code>PARTITION BY customer_id</code> restarts the calculation per customer.<br><code>SUM(amount) OVER ()</code> with no partition spans everything, handy for share-of-total: <code>amount * 100.0 / SUM(amount) OVER ()</code>.<br><b>GROUP BY collapses rows, a window function does not</b> &mdash; <code>GROUP BY customer_id</code> on four transactions for one customer returns one row, while <code>SUM(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id)</code> on the same four transactions still returns four rows, each carrying that customer's total alongside its own original values.</p><p class=\"note\"><b>Note</b><br>Window functions logically run after WHERE, GROUP BY and HAVING have already shaped the rows.<br>That's exactly why you cannot reference a window function's result directly inside a WHERE clause in the same query &mdash; wrap it in a CTE first if you need to filter on it.</p><p><b>Worked example:</b> a running total per customer, without collapsing rows:</p><div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>SELECT transaction_id, txn_date, amount,\n       SUM(amount) OVER (ORDER BY txn_date, transaction_id) AS running_total\nFROM transactions\nWHERE customer_id = 1\nORDER BY txn_date, transaction_id;</pre></div><p>Customer 1's four transactions, ordered by date, are 38.05, 181.54, 89.52 and 218.5.<br>The running_total column reads 38.05, then 219.59, then 309.11, then 527.61 on the last row &mdash; each row keeps its own value plus everything before it.</p><p>Running totals and share-of-total are core to trend and contribution analysis.</p>", "Ranking and deduplication": "<p><b>ROW_NUMBER, RANK, DENSE_RANK</b><br><code>ROW_NUMBER()</code> assigns a unique sequential number per window.<br><code>RANK()</code> and <code>DENSE_RANK()</code> handle ties differently.<br>Given amounts 100, 100, 90 ordered descending: ROW_NUMBER arbitrarily hands out 1, 2, 3, so you need a full tiebreaker (like adding <code>transaction_id</code> to the ORDER BY) if the result must be deterministic.<br>RANK gives 1, 1, 3, leaving a gap where the tie ate a rank.<br>DENSE_RANK gives 1, 1, 2, with no gap.<br>The key pattern is <b>keeping the latest row per group</b>: number rows with <code>ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY txn_date DESC)</code> then keep where the number is 1.<br>Because you cannot filter a window function in WHERE, wrap it in a CTE first.<br>In warehouses like BigQuery or Snowflake the shorthand is <code>QUALIFY ROW_NUMBER() OVER (...) = 1</code>.</p><p class=\"note\"><b>Note</b><br>For the &ldquo;keep just the latest row per group&rdquo; pattern specifically, <code>ROW_NUMBER()</code> is almost always the right choice over RANK or DENSE_RANK.<br>You want exactly one row per group regardless of ties, and only ROW_NUMBER guarantees that.</p><p><b>Worked example:</b> tag each customer's transactions with a rank, latest first:</p><div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>SELECT transaction_id, txn_date, amount,\n       ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY txn_date DESC, transaction_id DESC) AS rn\nFROM transactions\nWHERE customer_id = 1;</pre></div><p>Customer 1's four transactions dated 2024-05-03, 2024-06-09, 2024-07-18 and 2024-07-23 get rn 4, 3, 2 and 1.<br>Filtering to <code>WHERE rn = 1</code> keeps only the 2024-07-23 transaction (amount 218.5) &mdash; that customer's single latest purchase.</p><p>Deduplication and latest-state logic come up whenever transactional data has repeats.</p>", "LAG LEAD and period change": "<p><b>LAG and LEAD</b><br><code>LAG(col)</code> looks at a previous row and <code>LEAD(col)</code> at a following row, within the window order.<br>This is how you compare a period to the one before it.<br>LAG and LEAD are ordinary window functions, not a separate feature &mdash; they still need an <code>OVER (ORDER BY ...)</code> to define what &ldquo;previous&rdquo; and &ldquo;next&rdquo; even mean.<br>Adding <code>PARTITION BY</code> restarts the look-back per group, so month-over-month growth computed per customer wouldn't leak the last row of one customer into the first row of the next.<br>Both take an optional second argument for how many rows to look back or forward (<code>LAG(col, 2)</code> skips two rows) and an optional third argument supplying a default value instead of NULL for rows with nothing to compare to.<br>Month-over-month growth is <code>(rev - LAG(rev) OVER (ORDER BY month)) / LAG(rev) OVER (ORDER BY month)</code>.<br>Guard the division with <code>NULLIF(denominator, 0)</code>.</p><p class=\"note\"><b>Note</b><br>The first row in any window has no prior row, so LAG returns NULL there &mdash; that's expected, not a bug.<br>Always account for that NULL in downstream math instead of assuming every row has a comparison.</p><p><b>Worked example:</b> compare each month's revenue to the month before it:</p><div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>SELECT strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date) AS mth,\n       SUM(amount) AS revenue,\n       LAG(SUM(amount)) OVER (ORDER BY strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date)) AS prev_month_revenue\nFROM transactions\nGROUP BY mth\nORDER BY mth\nLIMIT 4;</pre></div><p>2024-02 revenue is 931.03 with no prior row, so prev_month_revenue is NULL there.<br>2024-03 revenue is 1102.14 and its prev_month_revenue is 931.03, so month-over-month growth is (1102.14-931.03)/931.03 &asymp; 18.4%.</p><p>Period-over-period change is the language of growth reporting.</p>", "NTILE and quantile segmentation": "<p><b>NTILE and PERCENT_RANK</b><br><code>NTILE(n)</code> splits ordered rows into n roughly equal buckets, so <code>NTILE(4) OVER (ORDER BY total DESC)</code> tags each customer with a spend quartile.<br><code>PERCENT_RANK()</code> gives a relative standing from 0 to 1.<br>These build segmentation frameworks directly: quartiles and quintiles of spend, frequency, or recency.<br>Add a tiebreaker to the ORDER BY (for example <code>ORDER BY total DESC, customer_id</code>) so bucket assignment is stable.<br>When the row count doesn't divide evenly among n buckets, NTILE distributes the leftover rows one at a time starting from bucket 1, so earlier buckets can end up one row larger than later ones.</p><p class=\"note\"><b>Note</b><br>NTILE only tells you which bucket a row falls into &mdash; it doesn't tell you how close a row is to the edge of its bucket.<br>Use <code>PERCENT_RANK()</code> instead when you need that finer relative standing.</p><p><b>Worked example:</b> split customers into spend quartiles:</p><div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>WITH spend AS (\n  SELECT customer_id, SUM(amount) AS total\n  FROM transactions\n  GROUP BY customer_id\n)\nSELECT customer_id, total,\n       NTILE(4) OVER (ORDER BY total DESC) AS quartile\nFROM spend\nORDER BY total DESC\nLIMIT 5;</pre></div><p>Customers 53 (3732.23), 19 (3176.7), 45 (2644.27) and 23 (2146.43) are the top spenders among 35 transacting customers, and NTILE(4) tags all of them quartile 1, the highest-spending quarter.</p><p>Behaviourally distinct cohorts often start as quantile cuts like these.</p>", "Date and time logic": "<p><b>Dates as text</b><br>Dates are text here in <code>YYYY-MM-DD</code>.<br><code>strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date)</code> truncates to month, the workhorse for monthly trends (the warehouse equivalent is often <code>DATE_TRUNC</code>).<br><code>date(signup_date, '+1 month')</code> does date math, and <code>julianday(a) - julianday(b)</code> gives the number of days between two dates.<br>Cast to int with <code>CAST(... AS INT)</code>.<br>Because dates are stored as plain text, they only sort and compare correctly <i>because</i> of the ISO format: zero-padded, most-significant part first, so text ordering and chronological ordering happen to agree.</p><p class=\"note\"><b>Note</b><br>That text-sorts-like-a-date trick would silently break under a format like <code>DD/MM/YYYY</code>, where sorting alphabetically would not sort by date at all.<br>Always confirm the stored date format before relying on plain text ordering.</p><p><b>Worked example:</b> truncate a date down to its month:</p><div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>SELECT transaction_id, txn_date, strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date) AS txn_month\nFROM transactions\nWHERE transaction_id = 1;</pre></div><p>transaction_id 1 has txn_date '2024-05-03', stored as plain text.<br>strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date) reformats that same text into '2024-05', so every transaction in May groups together regardless of which day it happened on.</p><p>Cohort windows, time-to-first-payment, and retention periods all rest on this.</p>", "Cohort retention": "<p><b>Cohort</b><br>A cohort groups customers by a shared start, usually signup month via <code>strftime('%Y-%m', signup_date)</code>.<br>Retention measures how many of each cohort are still active in later periods.<br>A cohort is simply &ldquo;everyone who started at the same time,&rdquo; so fair comparisons can be made between people who've had the same amount of time to behave.<br>A single blended retention number hides this: it can look fine while one specific cohort is actually churning hard.<br>The pattern: define each cohort and the month after signup, left join to distinct monthly activity, then divide retained customers by cohort size.<br>Use <code>COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id)</code> on both sides and guard the ratio.</p><p class=\"note\"><b>Note</b><br>The join direction matters more than usual here &mdash; it must be a <b>LEFT JOIN</b> from the cohort to later activity, not an INNER JOIN.<br>Customers who did <i>not</i> come back still need to appear as a row (with NULL activity) to be counted in the denominator; an INNER JOIN would silently drop them and inflate the retention rate.</p><p><b>Worked example:</b> size the January 2024 cohort, then check who came back the next month:</p><div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>WITH cohort AS (\n  SELECT customer_id, strftime('%Y-%m', signup_date) AS cohort_month\n  FROM customers\n  WHERE strftime('%Y-%m', signup_date) = '2024-01'\n),\nactive_next_month AS (\n  SELECT DISTINCT customer_id, strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date) AS txn_month\n  FROM transactions\n)\nSELECT COUNT(DISTINCT c.customer_id) AS cohort_size,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT a.customer_id) AS retained_m1\nFROM cohort c\nLEFT JOIN active_next_month a\n  ON a.customer_id = c.customer_id AND a.txn_month = '2024-02';</pre></div><p>9 customers signed up in January 2024, and 6 of them show up transacting again in February 2024 (the month right after signup), so retention_m1 is 6/9 = 0.67 &mdash; two thirds of that cohort returned the very next month.</p><p>Retention curves are one of the most requested consumer growth deliverables.</p>", "Funnel conversion and rate math": "<p><b>Funnel and conversion rate</b><br>A funnel counts distinct customers reaching each stage: signup, activated, first_payment, repeat_payment.<br>Conditional aggregation gets all stages in one row: <code>COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event_type='activated' THEN customer_id END)</code>.<br>Each stage is counted with <code>COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id)</code> rather than plain <code>COUNT(*)</code> because one customer can log the same event type more than once (two repeat_payment rows, for instance) &mdash; without DISTINCT, that customer would be counted twice at that stage and the conversion math would be wrong.<br>Conversion rates divide one stage by the previous.<br>The <code>FILTER (WHERE ...)</code> clause is a cleaner alternative to CASE inside aggregates.</p><p class=\"note\"><b>Note</b><br>Always wrap the denominator in <code>NULLIF(x, 0)</code> to avoid divide-by-zero, and multiply by <code>1.0</code> so integer division does not truncate.<br>An empty stage would otherwise crash the query instead of just returning NULL.</p><p><b>Worked example:</b> count distinct customers reaching each funnel stage:</p><div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>SELECT\n  COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event_type = 'signup' THEN customer_id END) AS signups,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event_type = 'activated' THEN customer_id END) AS activated,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event_type = 'first_payment' THEN customer_id END) AS paid\nFROM events;</pre></div><p>60 distinct customers signed up, 50 reached activated, and 37 reached first_payment.<br>Signup-to-activation is 50/60 &asymp; 0.83, and activation-to-payment is 37/50 = 0.74 &mdash; each stage's rate is that stage's distinct customers divided by the stage before it.</p><p>Activation, engagement, retention, and monetization rates are the funnel metrics named in the role.</p>", "Cross-product attachment": "<p><b>Attachment</b><br>Attachment measures how many distinct products a customer uses: <code>COUNT(DISTINCT product_id)</code> per customer.<br>Higher attachment usually means higher retention and value.<br>Attachment is about breadth <i>per customer</i>, not overall product popularity &mdash; it asks &ldquo;how many different products does this one customer use,&rdquo; not &ldquo;how many customers use this one product.&rdquo;<br>That's why the grouping key is <code>customer_id</code> and the thing being counted distinctly is <code>product_id</code>, the reverse of a typical popularity query.<br>To find customers who use a specific pair, filter to those products then require both: <code>WHERE product_id IN (1,3) GROUP BY customer_id HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT product_id) = 2</code>.</p><p class=\"note\"><b>Note</b><br>A customer with attachment of 1 is a single-product user and the most exposed to churn if that one product loses relevance.<br>A customer attached across several products is generally stickier.</p><p><b>Worked example:</b> count how many distinct products one customer has bought:</p><div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>SELECT customer_id, COUNT(DISTINCT product_id) AS distinct_products\nFROM transactions\nWHERE customer_id = 16\nGROUP BY customer_id;</pre></div><p>Customer 16 has transactions against product_id 1, 2, 4 and 5 &mdash; distinct_products comes back as 4.<br>A HAVING clause of <code>&gt;= 3</code> on the ungrouped-by-customer version of this query would keep them as a highly cross-sold customer.</p><p>Quantifying cross-product engagement across the portfolio is central to this team.</p>", "Subtotals with ROLLUP": "<p><b>ROLLUP and GROUPING SETS</b><br>Sometimes you want group rows plus subtotal and grand-total rows in one result.<br>Warehouses offer <code>GROUP BY ROLLUP(...)</code> and <code>GROUPING SETS(...)</code>.<br>SQLite does not, so the portable pattern is <code>UNION ALL</code>: one query for the grouped rows, another for the total.<br>You can also stack different groupings (by segment, then by country) into a single labelled result with UNION ALL, which mimics GROUPING SETS.</p><p class=\"note\"><b>Note</b><br>For UNION ALL to stack two queries into one result, both sides must return the same number of columns in the same order and compatible types.<br>That's why the grand-total query selects the literal string <code>'ALL'</code> in the position where the grouped query selects <code>segment</code> &mdash; it's a placeholder standing in for &ldquo;no particular segment, this row is everything.&rdquo;</p><p><b>Worked example:</b> SQLite has no ROLLUP, so stack a grand-total row with UNION ALL:</p><div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>SELECT c.segment, SUM(t.amount) AS revenue\nFROM transactions t\nJOIN customers c ON t.customer_id = c.customer_id\nGROUP BY c.segment\n\nUNION ALL\n\nSELECT 'ALL', SUM(amount)\nFROM transactions;</pre></div><p>Revenue by segment is Consumer 17235.86, Enterprise 3603.6 and SMB 8309.98.<br>The UNION ALL adds a fourth row labelled 'ALL' with 29149.44 &mdash; the same three segment rows, plus one extra row confirming they sum to the whole.</p><p>Executive summaries often need the breakdown and the total together.</p>", "Causal setup treatment and control": "<p><b>Treatment and control</b><br>Causal methods like difference-in-differences compare a <b>treatment</b> group against a <b>control</b> group, before and after an intervention.<br>The SQL job is building that base cleanly.<br>Tag each transaction as pre or post the campaign date with CASE, join to the group assignment, and aggregate into the four cells (group by period).<br>A per-customer pre and post total, plus group label, is the table a diff-in-diff or matching model consumes.<br>In plain terms: the treatment group received whatever is being tested (a campaign, a new feature, a price change) and the control group didn't, ideally assigned at random so the two groups start out comparable on everything else.</p><p class=\"note\"><b>Note</b><br>Compare pre-to-post <i>change</i> in each group, not just the post-period numbers, because some of the change would have happened anyway &mdash; seasonality, general growth, a currency swing &mdash; and that background trend should show up in the control group too.<br>Difference-in-differences works by subtracting the control's own pre-to-post change from the treatment's, which nets out that shared trend and isolates the effect of the treatment itself.</p><p><b>Worked example:</b> build the four treatment/control &times; pre/post cells:</p><div class=\"lesson-sql\"><pre>SELECT g.exp_group,\n       CASE WHEN t.txn_date &lt; '2024-04-01' THEN 'pre' ELSE 'post' END AS period,\n       AVG(t.amount) AS avg_amount,\n       COUNT(*) AS n\nFROM transactions t\nJOIN campaign g ON t.customer_id = g.customer_id\nGROUP BY g.exp_group, period;</pre></div><p>Before 2024-04-01, treatment and control average 135.88 and 135.42 per transaction &mdash; nearly identical, as a good pre-period should look.<br>After that date, control rises to 244.90 (n=61) while treatment rises to only 190.27 (n=64); comparing the size of each group's own pre-to-post change, not just the post-period numbers alone, is exactly what difference-in-differences measures.</p><p>The modelling is done in Python, but rigorous cohort construction in SQL is what makes it trustworthy.</p>"};
const QUESTIONS = [{"id": 0, "topic": "SELECT and DISTINCT", "diff": "easy", "ordered": false, "ask": "Let's warm up. Pull up the full customer table so we can see what we are working with.", "prompt": "Return every column for all rows in the customers table.", "hint": "The simplest query.", "solution": "SELECT * FROM customers;", "bonus": false}, {"id": 1, "topic": "SELECT and DISTINCT", "diff": "easy", "ordered": false, "ask": "I just need a quick directory-style view. Give me each customer's name and the country they are in.", "prompt": "Return only first_name and country from customers.", "hint": "Name the two columns.", "solution": "SELECT first_name, country FROM customers;", "bonus": false}, {"id": 2, "topic": "SELECT and DISTINCT", "diff": "easy", "ordered": false, "ask": "Marketing asks which acquisition channels actually appear in the data. What are the distinct channels?", "prompt": "Return the distinct acquisition channels customers came from.", "hint": "DISTINCT collapses duplicates.", "solution": "SELECT DISTINCT acquisition_channel FROM customers;", "bonus": false}, {"id": 182, "topic": "SELECT and DISTINCT", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should explain that SELECT chooses which columns to return from a table, and that column order in the output follows the order named in the SELECT clause, not the table's original layout. It should mention SELECT * as a quick way to return every column, useful for exploration but risky to leave in production code since it breaks if the table's schema changes. It should explain that DISTINCT removes duplicate rows from the result, and specifically that when DISTINCT is applied to multiple columns together, it collapses duplicate *combinations* of those columns, not each column independently. A strong answer might also mention aliasing columns with AS to rename output labels without changing the underlying data."}, {"id": 60, "topic": "SELECT and DISTINCT", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "A downstream tool expects our columns under different names. Relabel customer_id as id and first_name as name, keeping everything else.", "prompt": "Return every column from customers, aliasing customer_id to id and first_name to name.", "hint": "AS renames a column in the output only.", "solution": "SELECT customer_id AS id, first_name AS name, country, segment, signup_date, acquisition_channel FROM customers;", "bonus": true}, {"id": 61, "topic": "SELECT and DISTINCT", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Marketing wants to know which segment and channel combinations actually occur, not just each individually.", "prompt": "Return the distinct combinations of segment and acquisition_channel present in customers.", "hint": "DISTINCT applies across all listed columns together.", "solution": "SELECT DISTINCT segment, acquisition_channel FROM customers;", "bonus": true}, {"id": 62, "topic": "SELECT and DISTINCT", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "We're scoping an enterprise expansion. Which countries do we already have Enterprise customers in?", "prompt": "Return the distinct countries of customers in the Enterprise segment.", "hint": "Filter first, then apply DISTINCT to what's left.", "solution": "SELECT DISTINCT country FROM customers WHERE segment = 'Enterprise';", "bonus": true}, {"id": 63, "topic": "SELECT and DISTINCT", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "For a quick directory export, combine each customer's name and country into one readable string.", "prompt": "Return customer_id and a full_label column formatted like 'Ugo (UK)' using first_name and country.", "hint": "The || operator concatenates strings in SQLite.", "solution": "SELECT customer_id, first_name || ' (' || country || ')' AS full_label FROM customers;", "bonus": true}, {"id": 3, "topic": "Filtering with WHERE", "diff": "easy", "ordered": false, "ask": "We are planning a US-focused readout. Pull the customers based in the USA.", "prompt": "Return customer_id and first_name for customers based in the USA.", "hint": "Filter on country.", "solution": "SELECT customer_id, first_name FROM customers WHERE country = 'USA';", "bonus": false}, {"id": 4, "topic": "Filtering with WHERE", "diff": "easy", "ordered": false, "ask": "Finance wants to look at mid-sized payments. Show transactions where the amount sits between 100 and 500.", "prompt": "Return transaction_id and amount for transactions with an amount between 100 and 500 inclusive.", "hint": "BETWEEN a AND b.", "solution": "SELECT transaction_id, amount FROM transactions WHERE amount BETWEEN 100 AND 500;", "bonus": false}, {"id": 5, "topic": "Filtering with WHERE", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "We are sizing a push aimed at consumers we reached through paid and referral. Give me consumer-segment customers who came through Paid or Referral.", "prompt": "Return customer_id and first_name for Consumer-segment customers acquired through Paid or Referral channels.", "hint": "Combine an equality with IN.", "solution": "SELECT customer_id, first_name FROM customers WHERE segment = 'Consumer' AND acquisition_channel IN ('Paid','Referral');", "bonus": false}, {"id": 183, "topic": "Filtering with WHERE", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should explain that WHERE filters individual rows before any grouping happens, based on a boolean condition. It should cover the standard comparison operators, combining conditions with AND/OR (and that AND binds before OR unless parentheses say otherwise), and IN as a shorthand for multiple OR-equality checks against a list. It should mention that NULL requires IS NULL / IS NOT NULL rather than = or != , since NULL is not equal to anything, including itself. BETWEEN and LIKE (with % and _ wildcards) for pattern matching are also worth mentioning."}, {"id": 64, "topic": "Filtering with WHERE", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "We've already covered the USA and UK in another report. Show everyone else.", "prompt": "Return customer_id and first_name for customers not based in the USA or UK.", "hint": "NOT IN excludes a list of values.", "solution": "SELECT customer_id, first_name FROM customers WHERE country NOT IN ('USA','UK');", "bonus": true}, {"id": 65, "topic": "Filtering with WHERE", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Finance now wants the transactions outside the normal 100 to 500 range, for a separate review.", "prompt": "Return transaction_id and amount for transactions with an amount NOT between 100 and 500.", "hint": "NOT BETWEEN is the inverse of BETWEEN.", "solution": "SELECT transaction_id, amount FROM transactions WHERE amount NOT BETWEEN 100 AND 500;", "bonus": true}, {"id": 66, "topic": "Filtering with WHERE", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "A support ticket asks for anyone whose first name starts with 'J', or ends in the letter 'a'.", "prompt": "Return first_name and country where first_name starts with 'J' or ends with 'a'.", "hint": "LIKE 'J%' matches a starting letter; LIKE '%a' matches an ending letter.", "solution": "SELECT first_name, country FROM customers WHERE first_name LIKE 'J%' OR first_name LIKE '%a';", "bonus": true}, {"id": 67, "topic": "Filtering with WHERE", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Growth wants to check paid acquisition specifically from the first quarter of 2024.", "prompt": "Return customer_id for customers with acquisition_channel Paid and signup_date between 2024-01-01 and 2024-03-31.", "hint": "Combine an equality check and a BETWEEN with AND.", "solution": "SELECT customer_id FROM customers WHERE acquisition_channel='Paid' AND signup_date BETWEEN '2024-01-01' AND '2024-03-31';", "bonus": true}, {"id": 6, "topic": "Sorting and limiting", "diff": "easy", "ordered": true, "ask": "Leadership always wants the headline numbers first. What are our five largest transactions?", "prompt": "Return transaction_id and amount for the 5 largest transactions, largest first.", "hint": "ORDER BY then LIMIT.", "solution": "SELECT transaction_id, amount FROM transactions ORDER BY amount DESC LIMIT 5;", "bonus": false}, {"id": 7, "topic": "Sorting and limiting", "diff": "easy", "ordered": true, "ask": "For a tenure analysis, who were the first ten customers to join?", "prompt": "Return first_name and signup_date for the 10 earliest-joining customers, earliest first.", "hint": "Ascending is default.", "solution": "SELECT first_name, signup_date FROM customers ORDER BY signup_date ASC, customer_id LIMIT 10;", "bonus": false}, {"id": 8, "topic": "Sorting and limiting", "diff": "medium", "ordered": true, "ask": "We already looked at the top five. Now show me the next five largest, so the sixth through tenth.", "prompt": "Return transaction_id and amount for the 6th through 10th largest transactions.", "hint": "LIMIT with OFFSET.", "solution": "SELECT transaction_id, amount FROM transactions ORDER BY amount DESC, transaction_id LIMIT 5 OFFSET 5;", "bonus": false}, {"id": 184, "topic": "Sorting and limiting", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should explain that ORDER BY sorts the final result set, ascending by default (ASC) or descending with DESC, and that it can sort by multiple columns in sequence (ties in the first column broken by the second, and so on). It should explain LIMIT restricts how many rows are returned, commonly paired with ORDER BY to answer 'top N' questions, and that without an ORDER BY, which rows a LIMIT returns is not guaranteed to be consistent across runs."}, {"id": 68, "topic": "Sorting and limiting", "diff": "medium", "ordered": true, "ask": "What's the single smallest transaction we've ever processed?", "prompt": "Return transaction_id and amount for the single smallest transaction, breaking ties by the lowest transaction_id.", "hint": "ORDER BY ascending, then LIMIT 1.", "solution": "SELECT transaction_id, amount FROM transactions ORDER BY amount ASC, transaction_id ASC LIMIT 1;", "bonus": true}, {"id": 69, "topic": "Sorting and limiting", "diff": "medium", "ordered": true, "ask": "For a printed directory, list customers alphabetically by country, and within each country reverse-alphabetically by name.", "prompt": "Return first_name and country ordered by country ascending, then first_name descending.", "hint": "Each column in ORDER BY can have its own direction.", "solution": "SELECT first_name, country FROM customers ORDER BY country ASC, first_name DESC;", "bonus": true}, {"id": 70, "topic": "Sorting and limiting", "diff": "hard", "ordered": true, "ask": "Skip our newest signup (already featured elsewhere) and show the next three most recent.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, first_name and signup_date for the 3 most recently signed-up customers, excluding the single most recent.", "hint": "OFFSET 1 skips the first row of the sorted result.", "solution": "SELECT customer_id, first_name, signup_date FROM customers ORDER BY signup_date DESC LIMIT 3 OFFSET 1;", "bonus": true}, {"id": 71, "topic": "Sorting and limiting", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "What was our second-biggest transaction of all time?", "prompt": "Return transaction_id and amount for the second largest transaction only.", "hint": "LIMIT 1 OFFSET 1 skips the top row.", "solution": "SELECT transaction_id, amount FROM transactions ORDER BY amount DESC LIMIT 1 OFFSET 1;", "bonus": true}, {"id": 9, "topic": "Aggregation and COUNT DISTINCT", "diff": "easy", "ordered": false, "ask": "Quick sanity check before we go further: how many customers are in the base?", "prompt": "Return the total number of customers as customer_count.", "hint": "COUNT(*).", "solution": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS customer_count FROM customers;", "bonus": false}, {"id": 10, "topic": "Aggregation and COUNT DISTINCT", "diff": "easy", "ordered": false, "ask": "For the monetization snapshot, what is the total and the average value of completed transactions?", "prompt": "Return the total and average amount of completed transactions, rounded to 2 decimals, as total_amount and avg_amount.", "hint": "SUM and AVG with a WHERE, wrapped in ROUND.", "solution": "SELECT ROUND(SUM(amount),2) AS total_amount, ROUND(AVG(amount),2) AS avg_amount FROM transactions WHERE status = 'completed';", "bonus": false}, {"id": 11, "topic": "Aggregation and COUNT DISTINCT", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "How many customers are actually active, meaning they have transacted at least once?", "prompt": "Return the number of distinct customers who have made at least one transaction, as active_customers.", "hint": "COUNT(DISTINCT ...).", "solution": "SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id) AS active_customers FROM transactions;", "bonus": false}, {"id": 185, "topic": "Aggregation and COUNT DISTINCT", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should cover the core aggregate functions - COUNT, SUM, AVG, MIN, MAX - and that they collapse many rows into a single summary value. It should specifically explain the difference between COUNT(*) (counts all rows, including ones with NULLs in any column) and COUNT(column) (counts only rows where that specific column is not NULL), and that COUNT(DISTINCT column) counts unique non-null values rather than every row. It should note that aggregate functions ignore NULLs in their input column entirely, which affects AVG in particular."}, {"id": 72, "topic": "Aggregation and COUNT DISTINCT", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "How many different countries do we actually operate in?", "prompt": "Return the number of distinct countries among customers, as country_count.", "hint": "COUNT(DISTINCT col) counts unique values.", "solution": "SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT country) AS country_count FROM customers;", "bonus": true}, {"id": 73, "topic": "Aggregation and COUNT DISTINCT", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Risk wants a headline number: how many customers have any failed or refunded transaction?", "prompt": "Return the number of distinct customers with a failed or refunded transaction, as at_risk_customers.", "hint": "Filter with IN, then COUNT DISTINCT the customer_id.", "solution": "SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id) AS at_risk_customers FROM transactions WHERE status IN ('failed','refunded');", "bonus": true}, {"id": 74, "topic": "Aggregation and COUNT DISTINCT", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "What's the smallest and largest completed transaction we've seen?", "prompt": "Return the minimum and maximum amount among completed transactions, as min_amount and max_amount.", "hint": "MIN() and MAX() can be used in the same SELECT.", "solution": "SELECT MIN(amount) AS min_amount, MAX(amount) AS max_amount FROM transactions WHERE status='completed';", "bonus": true}, {"id": 75, "topic": "Aggregation and COUNT DISTINCT", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "How many unique customer-and-product combinations have ever transacted, regardless of how many times?", "prompt": "Return the count of distinct customer_id and product_id pairs in transactions, as distinct_pairs.", "hint": "Concatenate the two columns before counting distinct values.", "solution": "SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id || '-' || product_id) AS distinct_pairs FROM transactions;", "bonus": true}, {"id": 12, "topic": "GROUP BY", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Product wants revenue split by product. Sum the amount for each one.", "prompt": "Return product_id and total_amount: the summed amount per product, rounded to 2 decimals.", "hint": "Group by product_id.", "solution": "SELECT product_id, ROUND(SUM(amount),2) AS total_amount FROM transactions GROUP BY product_id;", "bonus": false}, {"id": 13, "topic": "GROUP BY", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Give me a customer count broken out by country and segment, so we can see where each segment concentrates.", "prompt": "Return country, segment and customer_count: the number of customers in each country and segment combination.", "hint": "Group by two columns.", "solution": "SELECT country, segment, COUNT(*) AS customer_count FROM customers GROUP BY country, segment;", "bonus": false}, {"id": 14, "topic": "GROUP BY", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "For a value analysis, per customer, how many completed transactions do they have and what is their completed revenue?", "prompt": "Return customer_id, txns and revenue: the count and rounded total amount of each customer's completed transactions.", "hint": "Filter to completed, then group.", "solution": "SELECT customer_id, COUNT(*) AS txns, ROUND(SUM(amount),2) AS revenue FROM transactions WHERE status='completed' GROUP BY customer_id;", "bonus": false}, {"id": 186, "topic": "GROUP BY", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should explain that GROUP BY splits rows into buckets based on one or more column values, and that any aggregate function in the SELECT list (COUNT, SUM, AVG, etc.) is then computed separately within each bucket rather than across the whole table. It should mention the rule that every non-aggregated column in the SELECT list must appear in the GROUP BY clause (or be functionally dependent on it), and that GROUP BY happens after WHERE filtering but before ORDER BY."}, {"id": 76, "topic": "GROUP BY", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Does spend behavior differ by how a customer was acquired? Join in the channel and average their completed transactions.", "prompt": "Return acquisition_channel and avg_amount: the average completed transaction amount by the customer's acquisition channel, rounded to 2 decimals.", "hint": "Join transactions to customers before grouping.", "solution": "SELECT c.acquisition_channel, ROUND(AVG(t.amount),2) AS avg_amount FROM transactions t JOIN customers c ON t.customer_id=c.customer_id WHERE t.status='completed' GROUP BY c.acquisition_channel;", "bonus": true}, {"id": 77, "topic": "GROUP BY", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Give ops a full breakdown of transaction outcomes: how many, and how much, per status.", "prompt": "Return status, count and total_amount: the count and rounded total amount of transactions per status.", "hint": "Two aggregates can be computed in the same GROUP BY query.", "solution": "SELECT status, COUNT(*) AS count, ROUND(SUM(amount),2) AS total_amount FROM transactions GROUP BY status;", "bonus": true}, {"id": 78, "topic": "GROUP BY", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "For a customer health dashboard, show how many distinct products and how many total transactions each customer has.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, num_products and num_txns: the distinct product count and total transaction count per customer.", "hint": "COUNT(DISTINCT ...) and COUNT(*) can both appear alongside a GROUP BY.", "solution": "SELECT customer_id, COUNT(DISTINCT product_id) AS num_products, COUNT(*) AS num_txns FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id;", "bonus": true}, {"id": 79, "topic": "GROUP BY", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Leadership wants signups broken down by year for a trend slide.", "prompt": "Return yr and customer_count: the number of customers grouped by signup year.", "hint": "strftime('%Y', col) extracts just the year.", "solution": "SELECT strftime('%Y', signup_date) AS yr, COUNT(*) AS customer_count FROM customers GROUP BY yr;", "bonus": true}, {"id": 15, "topic": "HAVING", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Which are our biggest markets? Show the countries with at least eleven customers.", "prompt": "Return country and customer_count for countries with 11 or more customers.", "hint": "HAVING filters after grouping.", "solution": "SELECT country, COUNT(*) AS customer_count FROM customers GROUP BY country HAVING COUNT(*) >= 11;", "bonus": false}, {"id": 16, "topic": "HAVING", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "We want to define a high-value group. Who has spent more than 500 in total?", "prompt": "Return customer_id and total_amount for customers whose total transacted amount exceeds 500, rounded to 2 decimals.", "hint": "HAVING SUM(amount) > 500.", "solution": "SELECT customer_id, ROUND(SUM(amount),2) AS total_amount FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id HAVING SUM(amount) > 500;", "bonus": false}, {"id": 17, "topic": "HAVING", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Which products are really getting used? Show the ones with more than thirty transactions.", "prompt": "Return product_id and txn_count for products with more than 30 transactions.", "hint": "Count then filter the count.", "solution": "SELECT product_id, COUNT(*) AS txn_count FROM transactions GROUP BY product_id HAVING COUNT(*) > 30;", "bonus": false}, {"id": 187, "topic": "HAVING", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should explain that HAVING filters groups after aggregation, in contrast to WHERE, which filters individual rows before any grouping happens. It should give a concrete example of why WHERE can't do a HAVING's job - you can't reference an aggregate like COUNT(*) or SUM(x) in a WHERE clause, because those values don't exist yet at the row-filtering stage. The correct clause order (FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, SELECT, ORDER BY conceptually, even though SELECT is written first) is worth being able to state clearly."}, {"id": 80, "topic": "HAVING", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Who are our most transactionally active customers, based on completed purchases specifically?", "prompt": "Return customer_id for customers with more than 3 completed transactions.", "hint": "Use conditional aggregation inside HAVING, since a plain WHERE can't filter on a status count.", "solution": "SELECT customer_id FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id HAVING SUM(CASE WHEN status='completed' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) > 3;", "bonus": true}, {"id": 81, "topic": "HAVING", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Which products tend to sell at a premium, averaging more than 150 per transaction?", "prompt": "Return product_id and avg_amount for products whose average transaction amount exceeds 150.", "hint": "HAVING filters on the aggregate AVG(amount).", "solution": "SELECT product_id, ROUND(AVG(amount),2) AS avg_amount FROM transactions GROUP BY product_id HAVING AVG(amount) > 150;", "bonus": true}, {"id": 82, "topic": "HAVING", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "For enterprise sales planning, which countries already have a meaningful enterprise presence (2 or more customers)?", "prompt": "Return country and enterprise_count for countries with 2 or more Enterprise-segment customers.", "hint": "Filter to Enterprise first, then group and use HAVING on the count.", "solution": "SELECT country, COUNT(*) AS enterprise_count FROM customers WHERE segment='Enterprise' GROUP BY country HAVING COUNT(*) >= 2;", "bonus": true}, {"id": 83, "topic": "HAVING", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Which customers have racked up more than 50 in refunds total? Worth a closer look.", "prompt": "Return customer_id for customers whose total refunded amount exceeds 50.", "hint": "Sum only the refunded rows with a CASE inside SUM, then filter with HAVING.", "solution": "SELECT customer_id FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id HAVING SUM(CASE WHEN status='refunded' THEN amount ELSE 0 END) > 50;", "bonus": true}, {"id": 18, "topic": "CASE expressions", "diff": "easy", "ordered": false, "ask": "Tag each transaction as high or low value using 200 as the cutoff, so we can bucket them.", "prompt": "Return transaction_id, amount and a size column that is 'high' when amount is 200 or more, otherwise 'low'.", "hint": "CASE WHEN ... THEN ... ELSE ... END.", "solution": "SELECT transaction_id, amount, CASE WHEN amount >= 200 THEN 'high' ELSE 'low' END AS size FROM transactions;", "bonus": false}, {"id": 19, "topic": "CASE expressions", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Map each customer to a simple tier: top for Enterprise, mid for SMB, base for everyone else.", "prompt": "Return first_name and tier: 'top' for Enterprise, 'mid' for SMB, 'base' otherwise.", "hint": "Multiple WHEN branches.", "solution": "SELECT first_name, CASE WHEN segment='Enterprise' THEN 'top' WHEN segment='SMB' THEN 'mid' ELSE 'base' END AS tier FROM customers;", "bonus": false}, {"id": 20, "topic": "CASE expressions", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "For a reliability view, per customer, how many of their transactions completed versus failed?", "prompt": "Return customer_id, completed and failed: per customer, the count of completed and failed transactions using conditional aggregation.", "hint": "SUM(CASE WHEN ... THEN 1 ELSE 0 END).", "solution": "SELECT customer_id, SUM(CASE WHEN status='completed' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS completed, SUM(CASE WHEN status='failed' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS failed FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id;", "bonus": false}, {"id": 188, "topic": "CASE expressions", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should explain that CASE WHEN ... THEN ... ELSE ... END lets you compute conditional, row-by-row logic directly inside a SQL query, similar to an if/else chain, producing a new derived value per row. It should mention that conditions are evaluated in order and the first matching WHEN wins, that ELSE provides a default when no condition matches (and that omitting it produces NULL for unmatched rows), and that CASE expressions are commonly used to bucket continuous values into categories or to pivot logic without a separate lookup table."}, {"id": 84, "topic": "CASE expressions", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Simplify our three acquisition channels into just two broader buckets for a slide: digital vs human-driven.", "prompt": "Return customer_id and channel_group: 'digital' for Organic or Paid, 'human' for Referral.", "hint": "CASE WHEN ... IN (...) THEN ... works fine.", "solution": "SELECT customer_id, CASE WHEN acquisition_channel IN ('Organic','Paid') THEN 'digital' ELSE 'human' END AS channel_group FROM customers;", "bonus": true}, {"id": 85, "topic": "CASE expressions", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Ops wants a quick visual flag on any transaction that needs a second look.", "prompt": "Return transaction_id, amount, status and a flag column: 'review' when status is failed or refunded, otherwise 'ok'.", "hint": "CASE WHEN status IN (...) THEN 'review' ELSE 'ok' END.", "solution": "SELECT transaction_id, amount, status, CASE WHEN status IN ('failed','refunded') THEN 'review' ELSE 'ok' END AS flag FROM transactions;", "bonus": true}, {"id": 86, "topic": "CASE expressions", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "For a regional rollup, group every country into either Europe or North America.", "prompt": "Return country and a region column: 'NA' for USA, otherwise 'Europe'.", "hint": "A single CASE WHEN with an ELSE covers every other country.", "solution": "SELECT country, CASE WHEN country='USA' THEN 'NA' ELSE 'Europe' END AS region FROM customers;", "bonus": true}, {"id": 87, "topic": "CASE expressions", "diff": "hard", "ordered": true, "ask": "Sales wants accounts ranked by priority tier: Enterprise first, then SMB, then Consumer.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, segment and a priority column (1 for Enterprise, 2 for SMB, 3 for Consumer), ordered by priority.", "hint": "Compute the tier with CASE, then ORDER BY that same expression's alias.", "solution": "SELECT customer_id, segment, CASE WHEN segment='Enterprise' THEN 1 WHEN segment='SMB' THEN 2 ELSE 3 END AS priority FROM customers ORDER BY priority;", "bonus": true}, {"id": 21, "topic": "Joins", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "The transactions table only stores product ids. Bring in the readable product name next to each transaction and its amount.", "prompt": "Return transaction_id, product_name and amount by joining transactions to products.", "hint": "INNER JOIN on product_id.", "solution": "SELECT t.transaction_id, p.product_name, t.amount FROM transactions t JOIN products p ON t.product_id=p.product_id;", "bonus": false, "subtopic": "INNER JOIN"}, {"id": 22, "topic": "Joins", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Same revenue-by-product view, but leadership wants the product names, not the ids.", "prompt": "Return product_name and total_amount: rounded total revenue per product name.", "hint": "Join then group by product_name.", "solution": "SELECT p.product_name, ROUND(SUM(t.amount),2) AS total_amount FROM transactions t JOIN products p ON t.product_id=p.product_id GROUP BY p.product_name;", "bonus": false, "subtopic": "INNER JOIN"}, {"id": 23, "topic": "Joins", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "We suspect some people signed up and never transacted. Find the customers with no transactions at all.", "prompt": "Return customer_id and first_name for customers who have never made a transaction.", "hint": "LEFT JOIN and keep null matches.", "solution": "SELECT c.customer_id, c.first_name FROM customers c LEFT JOIN transactions t ON c.customer_id=t.customer_id WHERE t.transaction_id IS NULL;", "bonus": false, "subtopic": "LEFT JOIN"}, {"id": 140, "topic": "Joins", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Support wants a clean list: every completed transaction's amount alongside the name of the customer who made it.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, first_name and amount for every completed transaction, joining customers to transactions.", "hint": "INNER JOIN customers to transactions, then filter status='completed'.", "solution": "SELECT c.customer_id, c.first_name, t.amount FROM customers c JOIN transactions t ON c.customer_id=t.customer_id WHERE t.status='completed';", "bonus": false, "subtopic": "INNER JOIN"}, {"id": 141, "topic": "Joins", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Risk wants a per-customer count of failed or refunded transactions, and customers with zero of those should still show up as 0.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, first_name and bad_txn_count (count of failed or refunded transactions) for every customer, using a LEFT JOIN so customers with none still show 0.", "hint": "LEFT JOIN transactions, then COUNT(CASE WHEN status IN (...) THEN 1 END) so unmatched rows count as 0 instead of vanishing.", "solution": "SELECT c.customer_id, c.first_name, COUNT(CASE WHEN t.status IN ('failed','refunded') THEN 1 END) AS bad_txn_count FROM customers c LEFT JOIN transactions t ON c.customer_id=t.customer_id GROUP BY c.customer_id, c.first_name;", "bonus": false, "subtopic": "LEFT JOIN"}, {"id": 142, "topic": "Joins", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Finance wants total lifetime spend for every customer, but customers with no transactions should show 0, not be missing from the report entirely.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, first_name and total_spent for every customer, using a LEFT JOIN to transactions and COALESCE to turn missing sums into 0.", "hint": "LEFT JOIN keeps every customer; SUM(amount) over unmatched rows is NULL, so wrap it in COALESCE(...,0).", "solution": "SELECT c.customer_id, c.first_name, COALESCE(SUM(t.amount),0) AS total_spent FROM customers c LEFT JOIN transactions t ON c.customer_id=t.customer_id GROUP BY c.customer_id, c.first_name;", "bonus": false, "subtopic": "LEFT JOIN"}, {"id": 143, "topic": "Joins", "diff": "hard", "ordered": true, "ask": "Growth wants to pair up customers who share the same country for a regional referral campaign — each pair should appear exactly once.", "prompt": "Return customer_a, customer_b and country for pairs of different customers in the same country, using a self join. Avoid pairing a customer with themselves and avoid duplicate reversed pairs.", "hint": "Self join customers to customers on country, and use a.customer_id < b.customer_id to keep exactly one direction of each pair.", "solution": "SELECT a.first_name AS customer_a, b.first_name AS customer_b, a.country FROM customers a JOIN customers b ON a.country=b.country AND a.customer_id<b.customer_id ORDER BY a.country, a.customer_id, b.customer_id;", "bonus": false, "subtopic": "SELF JOIN"}, {"id": 144, "topic": "Joins", "diff": "hard", "ordered": true, "ask": "Ops suspects a bulk-import batch. Find every pair of customers who signed up on the exact same date.", "prompt": "Return customer_a, customer_b and signup_date for distinct pairs of customers who share a signup_date, using a self join.", "hint": "Self join on signup_date equality, with a.customer_id < b.customer_id to avoid duplicate/reversed pairs.", "solution": "SELECT a.first_name AS customer_a, b.first_name AS customer_b, a.signup_date FROM customers a JOIN customers b ON a.signup_date=b.signup_date AND a.customer_id<b.customer_id ORDER BY a.signup_date, a.customer_id, b.customer_id;", "bonus": false, "subtopic": "SELF JOIN"}, {"id": 145, "topic": "Joins", "diff": "hard", "ordered": true, "ask": "For every Enterprise customer, find every other Enterprise customer who joined earlier, so account management can highlight longer-tenured peers.", "prompt": "Return newer_customer, earlier_customer and the earlier customer's signup_date for pairs of Enterprise customers where one signed up before the other, using a self join restricted to segment='Enterprise'.", "hint": "Self join customers to customers twice-filtered on segment='Enterprise', with b.signup_date < a.signup_date so b is always the earlier one.", "solution": "SELECT a.first_name AS newer_customer, b.first_name AS earlier_customer, b.signup_date FROM customers a JOIN customers b ON a.segment='Enterprise' AND b.segment='Enterprise' AND a.customer_id<>b.customer_id AND b.signup_date<a.signup_date ORDER BY a.customer_id, b.signup_date;", "bonus": false, "subtopic": "SELF JOIN"}, {"id": 189, "topic": "Joins", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should explain that joins combine rows from two or more tables based on a matching condition, usually a shared key. It should distinguish INNER JOIN (only rows with a match in both tables), LEFT JOIN (all rows from the left table, with NULLs filling in for any unmatched right-table columns), and mention RIGHT JOIN and FULL JOIN as the mirrored and combined cases. It should flag the classic pitfall of a join condition that unintentionally matches multiple rows on one side, silently multiplying (fan-out) the result set beyond what was intended, and note that filtering conditions on the right-hand table belong in the ON clause versus WHERE clause differently depending on whether you want to preserve unmatched left-side rows in a LEFT JOIN."}, {"id": 88, "topic": "Joins", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Build a clean receipt-style view: customer name alongside the product name, for every completed purchase.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, first_name and product_name for every completed transaction, joining customers, transactions and products.", "hint": "This needs two JOINs chained together.", "solution": "SELECT c.customer_id, c.first_name, p.product_name FROM customers c JOIN transactions t ON c.customer_id=t.customer_id JOIN products p ON t.product_id=p.product_id WHERE t.status='completed';", "bonus": true, "subtopic": "INNER JOIN"}, {"id": 89, "topic": "Joins", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "How many distinct customers has each product actually reached?", "prompt": "Return product_name and customer_count: the number of distinct customers who transacted in each product.", "hint": "Join transactions to products, then COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id) per product.", "solution": "SELECT p.product_name, COUNT(DISTINCT t.customer_id) AS customer_count FROM transactions t JOIN products p ON t.product_id=p.product_id GROUP BY p.product_name;", "bonus": true, "subtopic": "INNER JOIN"}, {"id": 90, "topic": "Joins", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "The causal team needs every customer's name alongside their treatment or control assignment.", "prompt": "Return first_name and exp_group for every customer, joining to the campaign table.", "hint": "A simple inner join on customer_id.", "solution": "SELECT c.first_name, g.exp_group FROM customers c JOIN campaign g ON c.customer_id=g.customer_id;", "bonus": true, "subtopic": "INNER JOIN"}, {"id": 91, "topic": "Joins", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "For an enterprise account review, show which products our Enterprise customers have actually completed a purchase in.", "prompt": "Return first_name and product_name for Enterprise-segment customers with a completed transaction, joining all three tables.", "hint": "Filter on segment after joining customers, transactions and products together.", "solution": "SELECT c.first_name, p.product_name FROM customers c JOIN transactions t ON c.customer_id=t.customer_id JOIN products p ON t.product_id=p.product_id WHERE c.segment='Enterprise' AND t.status='completed';", "bonus": true, "subtopic": "INNER JOIN"}, {"topic": "Subqueries", "diff": "easy", "ask": "Which transaction has the single highest amount overall?", "prompt": "Return transaction_id and amount for the transaction with the maximum amount.", "hint": "Scalar subquery with MAX() in WHERE.", "solution": "SELECT transaction_id, amount FROM transactions WHERE amount = (SELECT MAX(amount) FROM transactions);", "ordered": false, "id": 146, "bonus": false}, {"topic": "Subqueries", "diff": "medium", "ask": "Which completed transactions are above the average completed-transaction amount? We only care about completed payments, not failed or refunded ones.", "prompt": "Return transaction_id and amount for completed transactions above the average amount of completed transactions.", "hint": "Filter both the outer query and the subquery to status='completed'.", "solution": "SELECT transaction_id, amount FROM transactions WHERE status='completed' AND amount > (SELECT AVG(amount) FROM transactions WHERE status='completed') ORDER BY transaction_id;", "ordered": false, "id": 147, "bonus": false, "clues": "<p>A scalar subquery in WHERE only works if it returns exactly one number - <code>(SELECT AVG(amount) FROM transactions WHERE status='completed')</code>.</p><p>Filter <b>both</b> the outer query's rows and the subquery's rows to <code>status='completed'</code> - if you only filter one side, you're comparing completed transactions against an average that includes failed/refunded ones.</p>"}, {"topic": "Subqueries", "diff": "hard", "ask": "Among our Enterprise customers, who has spent (completed transactions only) more than the average Enterprise customer's total spend? Enterprise accounts are our highest-value segment, so leadership wants to know who's over-performing within that group.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, first_name and total_spend for Enterprise customers whose completed-transaction total exceeds the average total spend among Enterprise customers.", "hint": "Build each Enterprise customer's total in a GROUP BY, then compare it to a subquery averaging those same per-customer totals.", "solution": "SELECT c.customer_id, c.first_name, ROUND(SUM(t.amount),2) AS total_spend FROM transactions t JOIN customers c ON t.customer_id=c.customer_id WHERE c.segment='Enterprise' AND t.status='completed' GROUP BY c.customer_id, c.first_name HAVING SUM(t.amount) > (SELECT AVG(seg_total) FROM (SELECT SUM(t2.amount) AS seg_total FROM transactions t2 JOIN customers c2 ON t2.customer_id=c2.customer_id WHERE c2.segment='Enterprise' AND t2.status='completed' GROUP BY c2.customer_id)) ORDER BY total_spend DESC;", "ordered": false, "id": 148, "bonus": false, "clues": "<p>You're comparing a <b>per-customer total</b> against the <b>average of those same per-customer totals</b> - that's two different levels of aggregation, so you can't do it in one GROUP BY.</p><p>Build the per-customer totals first, restricted to Enterprise + completed, using GROUP BY with a HAVING for the comparison.</p><p>The benchmark itself (\"the average Enterprise customer's total\") needs its own aggregation over a derived table: <code>(SELECT AVG(x) FROM (SELECT SUM(...) AS x FROM ... GROUP BY customer_id) )</code>.</p><p>A plain <code>AVG(amount)</code> would average individual transaction amounts, not customer totals - that's the classic trap this question is testing.</p>"}, {"id": 190, "topic": "Subqueries", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should explain that a subquery is a query nested inside another query, and should distinguish the main patterns: a subquery in the WHERE clause (often with IN, EXISTS, or a scalar comparison) to filter based on another table's results; a scalar subquery used directly as a computed value; and a subquery in the FROM clause acting as a temporary derived table. It should mention EXISTS/NOT EXISTS as often more efficient and more NULL-safe than IN/ NOT IN for checking related-row presence, since NOT IN can behave unexpectedly if the subquery's result contains NULL."}, {"id": 24, "topic": "Subqueries", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Which transactions are above the average transaction size? We want to look at the larger-than-typical payments.", "prompt": "Return transaction_id and amount for transactions above the overall average transaction amount.", "hint": "Scalar subquery in WHERE.", "solution": "SELECT transaction_id, amount FROM transactions WHERE amount > (SELECT AVG(amount) FROM transactions);", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>A scalar subquery goes right inside the WHERE condition, in place of a number: <code>WHERE amount &gt; (SELECT AVG(amount) FROM transactions)</code>.</p><p>No GROUP BY needed here - you're comparing each row directly to one single benchmark value.</p>"}, {"id": 25, "topic": "Subqueries", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Risk flagged refunds this week. Which customers have had at least one refunded transaction?", "prompt": "Return customer_id and first_name for customers who have at least one refunded transaction.", "hint": "IN with a subquery.", "solution": "SELECT customer_id, first_name FROM customers WHERE customer_id IN (SELECT customer_id FROM transactions WHERE status='refunded');", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p><code>IN (SELECT ...)</code> checks whether a value appears anywhere in the list the subquery returns.</p><p>The subquery just needs one column: customer_id, filtered to refunded transactions - the outer query then filters customers to that list.</p>"}, {"id": 26, "topic": "Subqueries", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Show me the customers who have made at least one payment above 300.", "prompt": "Return customer_id and first_name for customers who have any transaction above 300.", "hint": "EXISTS correlated to the customer.", "solution": "SELECT customer_id, first_name FROM customers c WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM transactions t WHERE t.customer_id=c.customer_id AND t.amount>300);", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p><code>EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM ... WHERE ...)</code> checks whether at least one matching row exists - it doesn't care what column you select inside it, <code>1</code> is a common convention.</p><p>Make it correlated by referencing the outer customer's id inside the subquery's WHERE, e.g. <code>t.customer_id = c.customer_id</code> - that's what ties each customer to their own transactions.</p>"}, {"id": 92, "topic": "Subqueries", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Find customers spending above the AVERAGE customer's total spend, not the average transaction.", "prompt": "Return customer_id for customers whose total spend exceeds the average total spend per customer.", "hint": "Build the per-customer totals in a subquery, then average that subquery in a second subquery.", "solution": "SELECT customer_id FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id HAVING SUM(amount) > (SELECT AVG(t) FROM (SELECT SUM(amount) AS t FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id));", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Two levels of aggregation: build the per-customer totals first (GROUP BY customer_id, SUM(amount)), then compare each one to the average <i>of those totals</i>.</p><p>The benchmark itself needs its own subquery wrapping a derived table: <code>(SELECT AVG(t) FROM (SELECT SUM(amount) AS t FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id))</code> - a plain <code>AVG(amount)</code> would average transactions, not customer totals.</p>"}, {"id": 93, "topic": "Subqueries", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Which products have a completely clean track record, with zero failed transactions ever?", "prompt": "Return the distinct product_id values that have never had a failed transaction.", "hint": "NOT EXISTS checks that no matching failed row exists for that product.", "solution": "SELECT DISTINCT product_id FROM transactions p WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM transactions f WHERE f.product_id=p.product_id AND f.status='failed');", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p><code>NOT EXISTS</code> is the mirror image of EXISTS - it's true when <b>no</b> matching row is found.</p><p>Correlate the inner query to the outer product's id, filtered to <code>status='failed'</code> - if that inner query finds nothing for a given product, NOT EXISTS keeps that product.</p>"}, {"id": 94, "topic": "Subqueries", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Customer 1 is our benchmark. Who has strictly more transactions than they do?", "prompt": "Return customer_id and first_name for customers with more transactions than customer_id 1.", "hint": "A correlated subquery can count each customer's transactions and compare to a second subquery for customer 1.", "solution": "SELECT customer_id, first_name FROM customers c WHERE (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM transactions t WHERE t.customer_id=c.customer_id) > (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM transactions WHERE customer_id=1);", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>You need two separate counts here: each customer's own transaction count (correlated subquery, re-running per row), and customer 1's transaction count specifically (a second, non-correlated subquery).</p><p>Compare them directly in the WHERE clause: <code>(SELECT COUNT(*) FROM transactions WHERE customer_id=c.customer_id) &gt; (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM transactions WHERE customer_id=1)</code>.</p>"}, {"id": 95, "topic": "Subqueries", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Without using window functions, find each product's single highest transaction.", "prompt": "Return transaction_id, amount and product_id for the highest transaction within each product.", "hint": "A subquery pairing (product_id, MAX(amount)) can be matched with IN.", "solution": "SELECT transaction_id, amount, product_id FROM transactions WHERE (product_id, amount) IN (SELECT product_id, MAX(amount) FROM transactions GROUP BY product_id);", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>SQLite lets you compare a <i>tuple</i> of columns against a subquery: <code>WHERE (product_id, amount) IN (SELECT product_id, MAX(amount) FROM transactions GROUP BY product_id)</code>.</p><p>The subquery finds each product's maximum amount; the outer query then matches full rows back to those (product_id, amount) pairs - that's how you get the highest transaction per product without a window function.</p>"}, {"topic": "CTEs", "diff": "easy", "ask": "Using a CTE for each customer's total spend, show just customer 1's total.", "prompt": "Define a CTE of per-customer total spend, then select customer_id and total for customer_id = 1.", "hint": "WITH totals AS (SELECT customer_id, SUM(amount) AS total FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id) ...", "solution": "WITH totals AS (SELECT customer_id, SUM(amount) AS total FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id) SELECT customer_id, total FROM totals WHERE customer_id = 1;", "ordered": false, "id": 149, "bonus": false}, {"topic": "CTEs", "diff": "medium", "ask": "Using a CTE for per-customer total spend, label each customer as 'high value' (over 800) or 'standard'.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, total, and a tier column ('high value' if total > 800 else 'standard'), using a CTE for the totals.", "hint": "Wrap the per-customer SUM in a CTE, then CASE on it in the outer SELECT.", "solution": "WITH totals AS (SELECT customer_id, SUM(amount) AS total FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id) SELECT customer_id, total, CASE WHEN total > 800 THEN 'high value' ELSE 'standard' END AS tier FROM totals ORDER BY total DESC;", "ordered": false, "id": 150, "bonus": false, "clues": "<p>Build the per-customer SUM in the CTE only - don't put the CASE inside the CTE.</p><p>The CASE belongs in the outer SELECT, reading the CTE's <code>total</code> column: <code>CASE WHEN total &gt; 800 THEN 'high value' ELSE 'standard' END</code>.</p>"}, {"topic": "CTEs", "diff": "hard", "ask": "Using two chained CTEs (first each customer's total spend, then that joined to their acquisition channel), find which channels have an average customer spend above the company-wide average customer spend. Marketing wants to know which channels bring in higher-value customers.", "prompt": "Return channel and avg_spend for acquisition channels whose average per-customer total spend exceeds the overall average per-customer total spend, using two chained CTEs.", "hint": "First CTE: per-customer totals. Second CTE: join those totals to customers and average by channel. Then filter against a subquery averaging the first CTE.", "solution": "WITH cust_totals AS (SELECT customer_id, SUM(amount) AS total FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id), channel_avg AS (SELECT c.acquisition_channel AS channel, AVG(ct.total) AS avg_spend FROM cust_totals ct JOIN customers c ON ct.customer_id = c.customer_id GROUP BY c.acquisition_channel) SELECT channel, ROUND(avg_spend,2) AS avg_spend FROM channel_avg WHERE avg_spend > (SELECT AVG(total) FROM cust_totals) ORDER BY avg_spend DESC;", "ordered": true, "id": 151, "bonus": false, "clues": "<p>This needs two stages, so plan it as two CTEs: <code>WITH a AS (...), b AS (...)</code>.</p><p>Stage one: each customer's total spend (no channel yet).</p><p>Stage two: join stage one to <code>customers</code> to bring in <code>acquisition_channel</code>, then AVG the totals grouped by channel.</p><p>The final filter (\"above the company-wide average\") needs to reference stage one again, not stage two - you're comparing a per-channel average against the overall per-customer average, so pull that overall figure with <code>(SELECT AVG(total) FROM a)</code>.</p>"}, {"id": 191, "topic": "CTEs", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should explain that a CTE (WITH ... AS (...)) defines a named, temporary result set that can be referenced later in the same query, making complex queries more readable by breaking them into named, sequential steps rather than nesting nested subqueries. It should mention that multiple CTEs can be chained, each able to reference the ones defined before it, and that a CTE is generally not materialized/cached automatically the way a real table is - it's re-evaluated conceptually each time it's referenced, depending on the database engine."}, {"id": 27, "topic": "CTEs", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Define our top spenders as anyone over 800 in total, and write it as a clean multi-step query so it is easy to follow.", "prompt": "Using a CTE of each customer's total spend, return customer_id and total_amount for customers who spent more than 800, rounded to 2 decimals.", "hint": "WITH spend AS (...) SELECT ... WHERE.", "solution": "WITH spend AS (SELECT customer_id, SUM(amount) AS total FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id) SELECT customer_id, ROUND(total,2) AS total_amount FROM spend WHERE total > 800;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>The CTE just holds the per-customer totals (GROUP BY customer_id, SUM(amount)).</p><p>The outer query then filters that named result like a normal table: <code>SELECT ... FROM spend WHERE total &gt; 800</code>.</p>"}, {"id": 28, "topic": "CTEs", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Instead of a fixed cutoff, show customers who spend more than the average customer. Talk me through it in steps.", "prompt": "Using CTEs, return customer_id and total_amount for customers whose total spend is above the average customer total spend, rounded to 2 decimals.", "hint": "One CTE for per-customer totals, compare to their average.", "solution": "WITH spend AS (SELECT customer_id, SUM(amount) AS total FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id) SELECT customer_id, ROUND(total,2) AS total_amount FROM spend WHERE total > (SELECT AVG(total) FROM spend);", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Same CTE as a fixed-cutoff version, but the cutoff itself becomes a subquery over the CTE: <code>WHERE total &gt; (SELECT AVG(total) FROM spend)</code>.</p><p>You can reference the same CTE name twice in one query - once in the main FROM, once inside the subquery.</p>"}, {"id": 29, "topic": "CTEs", "diff": "medium", "ordered": true, "ask": "Give me revenue by month. I want to see how the trend moves over time.", "prompt": "Using a CTE, return mth and revenue: monthly total revenue rounded to 2 decimals, ordered by month.", "hint": "strftime for month inside the CTE.", "solution": "WITH m AS (SELECT strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date) AS mth, SUM(amount) AS rev FROM transactions GROUP BY 1) SELECT mth, ROUND(rev,2) AS revenue FROM m ORDER BY mth;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p><code>strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date)</code> truncates each date down to its month - group by that expression, not the raw date.</p><p>The CTE just isolates \"revenue per month\"; ORDER BY the month column in the outer query so the trend reads chronologically.</p>"}, {"id": 96, "topic": "CTEs", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Chain two CTEs: first monthly revenue, then that revenue's overall average, to find above-average months.", "prompt": "Using two CTEs, return mth and revenue for months where revenue exceeds the overall average monthly revenue.", "hint": "The second CTE can select from the first.", "solution": "WITH monthly AS (SELECT strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date) AS mth, SUM(amount) AS rev FROM transactions GROUP BY 1), avgrev AS (SELECT AVG(rev) AS a FROM monthly) SELECT mth, ROUND(rev,2) AS revenue FROM monthly, avgrev WHERE rev > a;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Two chained CTEs: the first computes monthly revenue (GROUP BY the truncated month), the second computes a single average over that first CTE's revenue column.</p><p>The outer query can then read from both CTEs at once (comma-join them, or a plain JOIN with no ON), filtering to rows where the month's revenue beats that second CTE's single average value.</p>"}, {"id": 97, "topic": "CTEs", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Using a CTE for per-product revenue, isolate only the single top-earning product.", "prompt": "Using a CTE, return product_id and revenue for the product with the single highest total revenue.", "hint": "Compare each row's revenue to the MAX of the same CTE.", "solution": "WITH prodrev AS (SELECT product_id, SUM(amount) AS revenue FROM transactions GROUP BY product_id) SELECT product_id, ROUND(revenue,2) AS revenue FROM prodrev WHERE revenue = (SELECT MAX(revenue) FROM prodrev);", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Build per-product revenue in the CTE, then compare each row to the single highest value in that same CTE.</p><p><code>WHERE revenue = (SELECT MAX(revenue) FROM prodrev)</code> - referencing the CTE a second time inside a subquery is exactly the pattern from the CTEs lesson.</p>"}, {"id": 98, "topic": "CTEs", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Using a CTE of each customer's distinct product count, flag the more diversified buyers.", "prompt": "Using a CTE, return customer_id and num_products for customers using more than 2 distinct products.", "hint": "Build the per-customer count first, then filter the CTE's result.", "solution": "WITH pc AS (SELECT customer_id, COUNT(DISTINCT product_id) AS num_products FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id) SELECT customer_id, num_products FROM pc WHERE num_products > 2;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>The CTE computes <code>COUNT(DISTINCT product_id)</code> per customer - no filtering yet.</p><p>The filter for \"more diversified\" happens afterward, in the outer query's WHERE, reading the CTE's count column directly - no need for a second subquery here since you're not comparing against an aggregate of the CTE.</p>"}, {"id": 99, "topic": "CTEs", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Using a CTE of monthly signup counts, find the single strongest signup month.", "prompt": "Using a CTE, return mth and cnt for the month with the most signups.", "hint": "Compare each row's count to the MAX of the same CTE.", "solution": "WITH signups AS (SELECT strftime('%Y-%m', signup_date) AS mth, COUNT(*) AS cnt FROM customers GROUP BY 1) SELECT mth, cnt FROM signups WHERE cnt = (SELECT MAX(cnt) FROM signups);", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Group customers by signup month in the CTE to get a monthly signup count.</p><p>\"Single strongest month\" means comparing each row to the MAX of that same count column, exactly like the top-earning-product pattern: <code>WHERE cnt = (SELECT MAX(cnt) FROM signups)</code>.</p>"}, {"topic": "Window basics", "diff": "easy", "ask": "Show the first 10 transactions by date with a running total of amount across ALL transactions (not per customer).", "prompt": "Return transaction_id, txn_date, amount and running_total (a cumulative SUM over all transactions ordered by date), limited to the first 10 rows by date.", "hint": "SUM(amount) OVER (ORDER BY txn_date, transaction_id), then LIMIT 10.", "solution": "SELECT transaction_id, txn_date, amount, SUM(amount) OVER (ORDER BY txn_date, transaction_id) AS running_total FROM transactions ORDER BY txn_date, transaction_id LIMIT 10;", "ordered": true, "id": 152, "bonus": false}, {"topic": "Window basics", "diff": "medium", "ask": "For Enterprise customers only, show a running total of their combined completed-transaction spend over time.", "prompt": "Return transaction_id, txn_date, amount and running_total for Enterprise customers' completed transactions, running total ordered by date.", "hint": "Join to customers to filter by segment, then apply SUM(...) OVER (ORDER BY ...) across the filtered rows.", "solution": "SELECT t.transaction_id, t.txn_date, t.amount, SUM(t.amount) OVER (ORDER BY t.txn_date, t.transaction_id) AS running_total FROM transactions t JOIN customers c ON t.customer_id=c.customer_id WHERE c.segment='Enterprise' AND t.status='completed' ORDER BY t.txn_date, t.transaction_id;", "ordered": true, "id": 153, "bonus": false, "clues": "<p>Filter to Enterprise + completed with a JOIN <i>before</i> the window function runs - put the JOIN and WHERE in the main FROM/WHERE, not inside the OVER clause.</p><p>Once filtered, the running total is the same shape as the lesson example: <code>SUM(amount) OVER (ORDER BY txn_date, transaction_id)</code>.</p>"}, {"topic": "Window basics", "diff": "hard", "ask": "For every completed transaction, show what percent of that customer's COUNTRY total completed revenue this single transaction represents. This is for a regional contribution analysis.", "prompt": "Return country, transaction_id, amount and pct_of_country_revenue (amount as a percent of that country's total completed revenue), for every completed transaction, ordered by country then transaction_id.", "hint": "PARTITION BY country inside SUM(...) OVER (...), then amount*100.0/that window sum.", "solution": "SELECT c.country, t.transaction_id, t.amount, ROUND(t.amount*100.0/SUM(t.amount) OVER (PARTITION BY c.country),2) AS pct_of_country_revenue FROM transactions t JOIN customers c ON t.customer_id=c.customer_id WHERE t.status='completed' ORDER BY c.country, t.transaction_id;", "ordered": false, "id": 154, "bonus": false, "clues": "<p>Country isn't a column on transactions, so you'll need a JOIN to customers before you can partition by it.</p><p>The \"percent of total\" pattern is always the same shape: <code>value * 100.0 / SUM(value) OVER (PARTITION BY ...)</code>.</p><p>Whatever you PARTITION BY is the group the percentage is relative to - partition by country, not by anything else, or the percentages won't add up to 100 within each country.</p><p>Don't forget the status filter before the window function runs, or countries will include revenue that shouldn't count.</p>"}, {"id": 192, "topic": "Window basics", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should explain that a window function computes a value across a set of related rows (the 'window') without collapsing them into a single output row the way GROUP BY does - every input row still appears in the output, each now carrying its own window-computed value alongside it. It should cover the OVER() clause, PARTITION BY (which defines the window's grouping, analogous to GROUP BY but without collapsing rows), and ORDER BY inside OVER() (defining the order the window function processes rows in, relevant for running totals and ranking)."}, {"id": 30, "topic": "Window basics", "diff": "hard", "ordered": true, "ask": "Build a cumulative revenue line over time, so each row shows revenue to date.", "prompt": "Return txn_date, amount and running_total: a cumulative sum of amount ordered by txn_date then transaction_id.", "hint": "SUM(amount) OVER (ORDER BY ...).", "solution": "SELECT txn_date, amount, ROUND(SUM(amount) OVER (ORDER BY txn_date, transaction_id),2) AS running_total FROM transactions;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>A running total needs no PARTITION BY at all if it's meant to span every transaction, not per-customer.</p><p><code>SUM(amount) OVER (ORDER BY txn_date, transaction_id)</code> - the ORDER BY is what makes each row's value include everything before it.</p>"}, {"id": 31, "topic": "Window basics", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "For a contribution chart, show each product's revenue and what percent of total revenue it represents.", "prompt": "Return product_id, revenue and pct_of_total: each product's rounded revenue and its percent share of total revenue, rounded to 2 decimals.", "hint": "SUM(SUM(amount)) OVER () gives the grand total.", "solution": "SELECT product_id, ROUND(SUM(amount),2) AS revenue, ROUND(SUM(amount)*100.0/SUM(SUM(amount)) OVER (),2) AS pct_of_total FROM transactions GROUP BY product_id;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>\"Percent of total revenue\" needs the grand total as the denominator - that's <code>SUM(SUM(amount)) OVER ()</code>, a window function wrapped around an aggregate, with no PARTITION BY so it spans every group.</p><p>This only works because the outer SUM is already inside a GROUP BY per product - window functions can sit on top of an aggregated result.</p>"}, {"id": 32, "topic": "Window basics", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "For each transaction, put that customer's own average transaction size right next to it, so we can see how each payment compares.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, transaction_id, amount and cust_avg: each transaction alongside that customer's average transaction amount, rounded to 2 decimals.", "hint": "AVG(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id).", "solution": "SELECT customer_id, transaction_id, amount, ROUND(AVG(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id),2) AS cust_avg FROM transactions;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>No GROUP BY needed - every original transaction row stays, each one annotated with a number.</p><p><code>AVG(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id)</code> computes that customer's average across all their rows, without collapsing anything.</p>"}, {"id": 100, "topic": "Window basics", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "For each transaction, how big a share is it of that particular customer's total spend?", "prompt": "Return transaction_id, customer_id, amount and pct_of_customer: each transaction's percent of that customer's total spend, rounded to 2 decimals.", "hint": "PARTITION BY customer_id inside SUM() OVER (...) gives a per-customer denominator.", "solution": "SELECT transaction_id, customer_id, amount, ROUND(amount*100.0/SUM(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id),2) AS pct_of_customer FROM transactions;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Same percent-of-total shape as a product-level version, but partitioned by customer instead: <code>PARTITION BY customer_id</code> inside the SUM window.</p><p><code>amount * 100.0 / SUM(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id)</code> - the multiplication by 100.0 (not 100) avoids integer division truncating to zero.</p>"}, {"id": 101, "topic": "Window basics", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Within each product, how does each transaction's amount rank against the others?", "prompt": "Return product_id, transaction_id, amount and rank_in_product: the rank of each transaction's amount within its product, highest first.", "hint": "RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY product_id ORDER BY amount DESC).", "solution": "SELECT product_id, transaction_id, amount, RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY product_id ORDER BY amount DESC) AS rank_in_product FROM transactions;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p><code>RANK()</code> needs an ORDER BY inside its OVER clause to know what to rank by - here, amount descending.</p><p><code>PARTITION BY product_id</code> restarts the ranking for each product, so ranks don't carry over between products.</p>"}, {"id": 102, "topic": "Window basics", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Track how each customer's average transaction size evolves as they transact over time.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, txn_date, amount and running_avg: a running average of amount per customer ordered by txn_date, rounded to 2 decimals.", "hint": "AVG(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY txn_date, transaction_id) gives a running average.", "solution": "SELECT customer_id, txn_date, amount, ROUND(AVG(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY txn_date, transaction_id),2) AS running_avg FROM transactions;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>A \"running average\" is the same idea as a running total, just AVG instead of SUM: <code>AVG(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY txn_date, transaction_id)</code>.</p><p>PARTITION BY keeps each customer's running average separate from everyone else's; ORDER BY is what makes it accumulate row by row instead of averaging everything at once.</p>"}, {"id": 103, "topic": "Window basics", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Flag how far each transaction sits from the overall average, without collapsing the rows with GROUP BY.", "prompt": "Return transaction_id, amount and diff_from_avg: each amount minus the overall average amount, rounded to 2 decimals.", "hint": "AVG(amount) OVER () with no PARTITION BY spans the whole table.", "solution": "SELECT transaction_id, amount, ROUND(amount - AVG(amount) OVER (),2) AS diff_from_avg FROM transactions;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>\"Without collapsing rows\" is the tell that this needs a window function, not GROUP BY.</p><p><code>AVG(amount) OVER ()</code> with empty parentheses computes one single average across the whole table, attached to every row - subtract it from each row's own amount.</p>"}, {"topic": "Ranking and deduplication", "diff": "easy", "ask": "Rank all completed transactions by amount, highest first, using RANK(). Show the top 10.", "prompt": "Return transaction_id, amount and amount_rank (RANK() OVER ordering amount descending) for completed transactions, top 10 rows.", "hint": "RANK() OVER (ORDER BY amount DESC), then LIMIT 10.", "solution": "SELECT transaction_id, amount, RANK() OVER (ORDER BY amount DESC) AS amount_rank FROM transactions WHERE status='completed' ORDER BY amount DESC LIMIT 10;", "ordered": false, "id": 155, "bonus": false}, {"topic": "Ranking and deduplication", "diff": "medium", "ask": "Rank customers by total completed spend, but within their own SEGMENT rather than against everyone.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, segment, total and rank_in_segment (RANK() partitioned by segment, ordered by total descending), for every customer, ordered by segment then rank.", "hint": "Build per-customer totals with a segment column, then RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY segment ORDER BY total DESC).", "solution": "WITH totals AS (SELECT c.customer_id, c.segment, SUM(t.amount) AS total FROM transactions t JOIN customers c ON t.customer_id=c.customer_id WHERE t.status='completed' GROUP BY c.customer_id, c.segment) SELECT customer_id, segment, total, RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY segment ORDER BY total DESC) AS rank_in_segment FROM totals ORDER BY segment, rank_in_segment;", "ordered": false, "id": 156, "bonus": false, "clues": "<p>Build the per-customer, per-segment totals first (a JOIN plus GROUP BY, filtered to completed).</p><p><code>RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY segment ORDER BY total DESC)</code> then restarts the ranking fresh within each segment.</p>"}, {"topic": "Ranking and deduplication", "diff": "hard", "ask": "For each calendar month, find the single largest completed transaction that month. This feeds a 'biggest deal of the month' slide for leadership.", "prompt": "Return mth, transaction_id and amount for the largest completed transaction in each calendar month, ordered by month.", "hint": "ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY month ORDER BY amount DESC), then keep rn = 1 via a CTE.", "solution": "WITH tagged AS (SELECT transaction_id, txn_date, amount, strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date) AS mth, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date) ORDER BY amount DESC, transaction_id) AS rn FROM transactions WHERE status='completed') SELECT mth, transaction_id, amount FROM tagged WHERE rn=1 ORDER BY mth;", "ordered": false, "id": 157, "bonus": false, "clues": "<p>You can't filter a window function's result in the same SELECT it's computed in, so this needs a CTE: compute the row numbers first, then filter <code>WHERE rn = 1</code> in the outer query.</p><p>The partition key is the month, not the customer - you need <code>strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date)</code> both as an output column and inside <code>PARTITION BY</code>.</p><p>Order within each partition by amount descending so rank 1 is the largest; add <code>transaction_id</code> as a second ORDER BY key in case two transactions in the same month tie on amount.</p>"}, {"id": 193, "topic": "Ranking and deduplication", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should distinguish ROW_NUMBER() (always assigns a unique, sequential rank with no ties, arbitrary among true ties), RANK() (ties share the same rank, but the next rank skips ahead by the number of tied rows), and DENSE_RANK() (ties share the same rank, but the next rank does not skip). It should explain the common 'latest record per group' or 'top N per group' pattern: using ROW_NUMBER() PARTITION BY the group column, ORDER BY the tiebreak column, then filtering to row number 1 (or N) in an outer query, since window functions can't be filtered directly in the same WHERE clause they're computed in."}, {"id": 33, "topic": "Ranking and deduplication", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Rank our customers by total spend, highest first. This feeds a top-accounts list.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, total_amount and rnk: customers ranked by total spend from highest to lowest using RANK. Round total to 2 decimals.", "hint": "RANK() OVER (ORDER BY SUM(amount) DESC).", "solution": "SELECT customer_id, ROUND(SUM(amount),2) AS total_amount, RANK() OVER (ORDER BY SUM(amount) DESC) AS rnk FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Aggregate and window function can sit in the very same SELECT here: <code>SUM(amount)</code> and <code>RANK() OVER (ORDER BY SUM(amount) DESC)</code> together, with a GROUP BY customer_id.</p><p>The window function reads the same aggregated value it's ranking by - no CTE required for this one since there's nothing to filter afterward.</p>"}, {"id": 34, "topic": "Ranking and deduplication", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Rank the products by revenue, and use a dense ranking so tied products do not leave gaps in the numbers.", "prompt": "Return product_id, revenue and rnk: products ranked by revenue high to low using DENSE_RANK. Round revenue to 2 decimals.", "hint": "DENSE_RANK leaves no gaps on ties.", "solution": "SELECT product_id, ROUND(SUM(amount),2) AS revenue, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY SUM(amount) DESC) AS rnk FROM transactions GROUP BY product_id;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Same shape as ranking customers, but grouped by product instead, and with <code>DENSE_RANK()</code> in place of RANK().</p><p>The only difference between RANK and DENSE_RANK is what happens after a tie - DENSE_RANK doesn't leave a gap in the numbers.</p>"}, {"id": 35, "topic": "Ranking and deduplication", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "For a recency view, give me just the single most recent transaction for each customer.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, transaction_id, txn_date and amount for each customer's single most recent transaction (latest txn_date, breaking ties by highest transaction_id).", "hint": "Number rows per customer with ROW_NUMBER, keep number 1, using a CTE.", "solution": "WITH r AS (SELECT customer_id, transaction_id, txn_date, amount, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY txn_date DESC, transaction_id DESC) AS rn FROM transactions) SELECT customer_id, transaction_id, txn_date, amount FROM r WHERE rn=1;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>You can't filter a window function's output in the same SELECT that computes it, so this needs a CTE: number the rows first, then filter <code>WHERE rn = 1</code> in the outer query.</p><p><code>ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY txn_date DESC, transaction_id DESC)</code> - ordering by date descending means rn=1 is always the most recent row for that customer.</p>"}, {"id": 104, "topic": "Ranking and deduplication", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Instead of latest-per-customer, we now need latest-per-product for a freshness check.", "prompt": "Return product_id, transaction_id, txn_date and amount for each product's single most recent transaction.", "hint": "PARTITION BY product_id this time, not customer_id.", "solution": "WITH r AS (SELECT product_id, transaction_id, txn_date, amount, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY product_id ORDER BY txn_date DESC, transaction_id DESC) AS rn FROM transactions) SELECT product_id, transaction_id, txn_date, amount FROM r WHERE rn=1;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Exactly the same CTE-then-filter shape as latest-per-customer - just swap the PARTITION BY key from customer_id to product_id.</p><p>Everything else (ROW_NUMBER, ORDER BY date descending, filtering to rn=1) stays the same.</p>"}, {"id": 105, "topic": "Ranking and deduplication", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "For a cohort report, we need each customer's very first transaction date only.", "prompt": "Return customer_id and first_txn_date: each customer's earliest transaction date only.", "hint": "ROW_NUMBER() ordered ascending by date keeps the earliest row per customer.", "solution": "WITH r AS (SELECT customer_id, transaction_id, txn_date, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY txn_date ASC, transaction_id ASC) AS rn FROM transactions) SELECT customer_id, txn_date AS first_txn_date FROM r WHERE rn=1;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Same ROW_NUMBER-then-filter pattern, but order ascending this time - <code>ORDER BY txn_date ASC</code> - so rn=1 lands on the earliest row per customer instead of the latest.</p>"}, {"id": 106, "topic": "Ranking and deduplication", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Give leadership just the top 3 spenders, with their rank attached.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, total_amount and rnk for the top 3 customers by total spend, using ROW_NUMBER.", "hint": "Rank in a CTE, then filter the outer query to rnk <= 3.", "solution": "WITH s AS (SELECT customer_id, SUM(amount) AS total_amount FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id), r AS (SELECT customer_id, ROUND(total_amount,2) AS total_amount, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY total_amount DESC) AS rnk FROM s) SELECT customer_id, total_amount, rnk FROM r WHERE rnk <= 3;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Build the per-customer totals in one CTE, then rank them in a second CTE (or the same one, chained) before filtering.</p><p><code>ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY total_amount DESC)</code> gives each customer a rank; the outer query then keeps <code>WHERE rnk &lt;= 3</code>.</p>"}, {"id": 107, "topic": "Ranking and deduplication", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "For regional account coverage, identify the single highest-spending customer in each country.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, country and total for the single highest-spending customer in each country.", "hint": "PARTITION BY country in the ranking window, then keep rank 1 per partition.", "solution": "WITH s AS (SELECT c.customer_id, c.country, SUM(t.amount) AS total FROM customers c JOIN transactions t ON c.customer_id=t.customer_id GROUP BY c.customer_id, c.country), r AS (SELECT customer_id, country, total, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY country ORDER BY total DESC) AS rn FROM s) SELECT customer_id, country, ROUND(total,2) AS total FROM r WHERE rn=1;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Two-stage build: first each customer's total spend with their country attached (JOIN + GROUP BY), then <code>ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY country ORDER BY total DESC)</code> in a second CTE.</p><p>Filter to <code>rn = 1</code> in the outer query - that keeps exactly one customer per country, the highest spender.</p>"}, {"topic": "LAG LEAD and period change", "diff": "easy", "ask": "For the monthly revenue trend, show each month's completed revenue alongside the PRIOR month's revenue.", "prompt": "Return mth, revenue and prev_month_revenue (LAG of revenue ordered by month) for completed transactions, ordered by month.", "hint": "Group into monthly revenue first, then LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY mth).", "solution": "WITH m AS (SELECT strftime('%Y-%m',txn_date) AS mth, SUM(amount) AS revenue FROM transactions WHERE status='completed' GROUP BY 1) SELECT mth, ROUND(revenue,2) AS revenue, ROUND(LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY mth),2) AS prev_month_revenue FROM m ORDER BY mth;", "ordered": true, "id": 158, "bonus": false}, {"topic": "LAG LEAD and period change", "diff": "medium", "ask": "For Enterprise customers only, show month-over-month revenue change (this month's revenue minus last month's).", "prompt": "Return mth, revenue and change_vs_prev_month for Enterprise customers' completed transactions, grouped by month, ordered by month.", "hint": "Filter to Enterprise via a JOIN before grouping by month, then subtract LAG(revenue) from revenue.", "solution": "WITH m AS (SELECT strftime('%Y-%m', t.txn_date) AS mth, SUM(t.amount) AS revenue FROM transactions t JOIN customers c ON t.customer_id=c.customer_id WHERE c.segment='Enterprise' AND t.status='completed' GROUP BY 1) SELECT mth, ROUND(revenue,2) AS revenue, ROUND(revenue - LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY mth),2) AS change_vs_prev_month FROM m ORDER BY mth;", "ordered": true, "id": 159, "bonus": false, "clues": "<p>Filter to Enterprise via a JOIN before grouping by month - the segment filter has to happen before the monthly aggregation, not after.</p><p>Once you have the monthly revenue CTE, <code>revenue - LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY mth)</code> gives the change versus the previous month directly.</p>"}, {"topic": "LAG LEAD and period change", "diff": "hard", "ask": "For each customer's transactions in order, show the number of days since their PREVIOUS purchase, and flag any gap longer than 30 days as 'at risk'. This is an early-warning signal the retention team wants to monitor.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, transaction_id, txn_date, days_since_prev and a flag column ('at risk' if the gap since the previous transaction exceeds 30 days, else 'active'), for the first 15 rows ordered by customer_id then txn_date. Rows with no previous transaction should be excluded.", "hint": "LAG(txn_date) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY txn_date), then julianday() to get the day gap, then CASE on it.", "solution": "WITH ordered AS (SELECT customer_id, transaction_id, txn_date, LAG(txn_date) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY txn_date, transaction_id) AS prev_date FROM transactions) SELECT customer_id, transaction_id, txn_date, CAST(julianday(txn_date) - julianday(prev_date) AS INT) AS days_since_prev, CASE WHEN julianday(txn_date) - julianday(prev_date) > 30 THEN 'at risk' ELSE 'active' END AS flag FROM ordered WHERE prev_date IS NOT NULL ORDER BY customer_id, txn_date LIMIT 15;", "ordered": false, "id": 160, "bonus": false, "clues": "<p><code>LAG(txn_date) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY txn_date)</code> gives you the previous date for that same customer - build that in a CTE first.</p><p>To turn two dates into a day count, subtract their <code>julianday(...)</code> values and cast the result to an integer.</p><p>The 'at risk' label is just a CASE on that day-count expression - nothing fancy, just make sure it's computed after the LAG, in the outer query reading from the CTE.</p><p>Every customer's very first transaction has no previous row, so LAG returns NULL there - filter those rows out explicitly rather than letting the day-count math choke on a NULL.</p>"}, {"id": 194, "topic": "LAG LEAD and period change", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should explain that LAG() and LEAD() let a query look at a value from a previous or following row, within a defined PARTITION BY/ORDER BY window, without needing a self-join. It should mention the optional offset argument (how many rows back/forward) and default-value argument (what to return when there is no such row, e.g. the first row in a partition has no prior row for LAG). It should connect this to period-over-period comparisons - computing month-over-month or day-over-day change by subtracting a LAG'd prior value from the current row's value."}, {"id": 36, "topic": "LAG LEAD and period change", "diff": "hard", "ordered": true, "ask": "For the monthly trend, put last month's revenue next to each month so we can compare side by side.", "prompt": "Return mth, revenue and prev_revenue: monthly revenue and the previous month's revenue, both rounded to 2 decimals, ordered by month.", "hint": "LAG(rev) OVER (ORDER BY mth) in a CTE.", "solution": "WITH m AS (SELECT strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date) AS mth, SUM(amount) AS rev FROM transactions GROUP BY 1) SELECT mth, ROUND(rev,2) AS revenue, ROUND(LAG(rev) OVER (ORDER BY mth),2) AS prev_revenue FROM m ORDER BY mth;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Group into monthly revenue in a CTE first - LAG needs rows to already be one-per-month before it can look at \"the previous one.\"</p><p><code>LAG(rev) OVER (ORDER BY mth)</code> reads the revenue value from the row immediately before the current one in that ordering.</p>"}, {"id": 37, "topic": "LAG LEAD and period change", "diff": "hard", "ordered": true, "ask": "Now turn that into month-over-month growth as a percentage. This is the number leadership asks for in every review.", "prompt": "Return mth and mom_growth_pct: month-over-month revenue growth as a percentage rounded to 2 decimals, ordered by month. The first month is null.", "hint": "(rev - LAG(rev)) * 100 / NULLIF(LAG(rev),0).", "solution": "WITH m AS (SELECT strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date) AS mth, SUM(amount) AS rev FROM transactions GROUP BY 1) SELECT mth, ROUND((rev - LAG(rev) OVER (ORDER BY mth)) * 100.0 / NULLIF(LAG(rev) OVER (ORDER BY mth),0),2) AS mom_growth_pct FROM m ORDER BY mth;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Growth percentage is just the LAG pattern with arithmetic wrapped around it: <code>(rev - LAG(rev)) * 100.0 / LAG(rev)</code>, all ordered by month.</p><p>Wrap the denominator in <code>NULLIF(LAG(rev) OVER (...), 0)</code> so a zero-revenue prior month doesn't crash the division - and remember LAG's first row is NULL anyway, so growth is undefined for the very first month.</p>"}, {"id": 38, "topic": "LAG LEAD and period change", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "For a gap analysis, for each transaction show when that customer next transacted.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, txn_date and next_txn_date: for each transaction, the date of that customer's next transaction, or null if none.", "hint": "LEAD(txn_date) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY ...).", "solution": "SELECT customer_id, txn_date, LEAD(txn_date) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY txn_date, transaction_id) AS next_txn_date FROM transactions;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p><code>LEAD</code> is LAG's mirror image - it looks at a <i>following</i> row instead of a prior one.</p><p><code>LEAD(txn_date) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY txn_date, transaction_id)</code> - no CTE needed, this works directly on the raw transactions.</p>"}, {"id": 108, "topic": "LAG LEAD and period change", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "For each transaction, what was that same customer's previous transaction amount?", "prompt": "Return customer_id, txn_date and prev_amount: each transaction's previous amount for that customer.", "hint": "LAG(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY txn_date, transaction_id).", "solution": "SELECT customer_id, txn_date, ROUND(LAG(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY txn_date, transaction_id),2) AS prev_amount FROM transactions;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p><code>LAG(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY txn_date, transaction_id)</code> - partition so each customer's lookback only sees their own transactions.</p><p>No aggregation needed here, this runs directly against the transactions table row by row.</p>"}, {"id": 109, "topic": "LAG LEAD and period change", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Did this customer spend more or less than last time? Compute the actual change.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, txn_date and amount_change: the amount minus that customer's previous transaction amount.", "hint": "Subtract the LAG() value from the current row's amount.", "solution": "SELECT customer_id, txn_date, ROUND(amount - LAG(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY txn_date, transaction_id),2) AS amount_change FROM transactions;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Same LAG setup as \"previous transaction amount,\" just subtract it from the current row's amount: <code>amount - LAG(amount) OVER (...)</code>.</p>"}, {"id": 110, "topic": "LAG LEAD and period change", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "How long has it been since this customer's last purchase, at the time of each transaction?", "prompt": "Return customer_id, txn_date and days_since_prev: whole days since that customer's previous transaction.", "hint": "Combine LAG() with julianday() to get a day difference.", "solution": "SELECT customer_id, txn_date, CAST(julianday(txn_date) - julianday(LAG(txn_date) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY txn_date, transaction_id)) AS INT) AS days_since_prev FROM transactions;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Combine two things you've already used separately: <code>LAG(txn_date)</code> to get the previous date, and <code>julianday(...) - julianday(...)</code> to turn two dates into a day count.</p><p>The LAG has to be computed first (or inline) before you can subtract its julianday from the current row's julianday.</p>"}, {"id": 111, "topic": "LAG LEAD and period change", "diff": "hard", "ordered": true, "ask": "Alongside each month's revenue, show what the FOLLOWING month brought in, for forward-looking comparison.", "prompt": "Return mth, revenue and next_mth_revenue: monthly revenue and the following month's revenue, ordered by month.", "hint": "LEAD() looks ahead to a following row instead of a prior one.", "solution": "WITH m AS (SELECT strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date) AS mth, SUM(amount) AS rev FROM transactions GROUP BY 1) SELECT mth, ROUND(rev,2) AS revenue, ROUND(LEAD(rev) OVER (ORDER BY mth),2) AS next_mth_revenue FROM m ORDER BY mth;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Same monthly-revenue CTE as the month-over-month questions, just swap <code>LAG</code> for <code>LEAD</code> to look at the following month instead of the prior one.</p>"}, {"topic": "NTILE and quantile segmentation", "diff": "easy", "ask": "Split customers into spend quartiles using NTILE(4), highest spenders in quartile 1.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, total and quartile (NTILE(4) ordered by total descending) for each customer's total spend.", "hint": "Build per-customer totals, then NTILE(4) OVER (ORDER BY total DESC).", "solution": "WITH totals AS (SELECT customer_id, SUM(amount) AS total FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id) SELECT customer_id, total, NTILE(4) OVER (ORDER BY total DESC) AS quartile FROM totals ORDER BY total DESC;", "ordered": false, "id": 161, "bonus": false}, {"topic": "NTILE and quantile segmentation", "diff": "medium", "ask": "Within Enterprise customers only, split into spend tertiles (3 roughly equal groups).", "prompt": "Return customer_id, total and tertile (NTILE(3) ordered by total descending) for Enterprise customers' completed-transaction totals.", "hint": "Filter to Enterprise via a JOIN before building totals, then NTILE(3).", "solution": "WITH totals AS (SELECT c.customer_id, SUM(t.amount) AS total FROM transactions t JOIN customers c ON t.customer_id=c.customer_id WHERE c.segment='Enterprise' AND t.status='completed' GROUP BY c.customer_id) SELECT customer_id, total, NTILE(3) OVER (ORDER BY total DESC) AS tertile FROM totals ORDER BY total DESC;", "ordered": false, "id": 162, "bonus": false, "clues": "<p>Filter to Enterprise via a JOIN before building the per-customer totals - the segment filter belongs inside the CTE that builds the totals.</p><p><code>NTILE(3)</code> works exactly like NTILE(4) from the lesson, just with a different bucket count.</p>"}, {"topic": "NTILE and quantile segmentation", "diff": "hard", "ask": "Split all customers into spend quintiles, then summarize the average spend and customer count within each quintile, to show marketing the gap between the top and bottom quintile.", "prompt": "Return quintile, customers (count) and avg_spend for each of the 5 NTILE spend quintiles, ordered by quintile.", "hint": "First NTILE(5) in a CTE, then GROUP BY quintile in the outer query.", "solution": "WITH totals AS (SELECT customer_id, SUM(amount) AS total FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id), tiered AS (SELECT customer_id, total, NTILE(5) OVER (ORDER BY total DESC) AS quintile FROM totals) SELECT quintile, COUNT(*) AS customers, ROUND(AVG(total),2) AS avg_spend FROM tiered GROUP BY quintile ORDER BY quintile;", "ordered": true, "id": 163, "bonus": false, "clues": "<p>You need two layers here: first assign each customer to a quintile with NTILE, then separately summarize by quintile - you can't GROUP BY a window function's own output in the same SELECT it's produced in.</p><p>Layer one (a CTE): per-customer total spend, then <code>NTILE(5) OVER (ORDER BY total DESC)</code> alongside it.</p><p>Layer two (the outer query): GROUP BY the quintile column from layer one, then COUNT(*) and AVG(total) per group.</p>"}, {"id": 195, "topic": "NTILE and quantile segmentation", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should explain that NTILE(n) divides the rows within a window (partition/order) into n roughly equal-sized buckets, numbered 1 through n, commonly used to split customers or values into quartiles, quintiles, or deciles for segmentation. It should mention that when the row count doesn't divide evenly, NTILE distributes the remainder across the earliest buckets, so bucket sizes can differ by at most one row, and that which end (1 or n) represents 'best' or 'highest' depends entirely on the ORDER BY direction chosen inside the window."}, {"id": 39, "topic": "NTILE and quantile segmentation", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "We want to segment customers into spend quartiles. Assign each one a quartile, with one being the highest spenders.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, total and quartile: each customer's rounded total spend and their spend quartile (1 = highest), using NTILE(4).", "hint": "NTILE(4) OVER (ORDER BY total DESC, customer_id) in a CTE.", "solution": "WITH s AS (SELECT customer_id, SUM(amount) AS total FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id) SELECT customer_id, ROUND(total,2) AS total, NTILE(4) OVER (ORDER BY total DESC, customer_id) AS quartile FROM s;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Build per-customer totals in a CTE first - NTILE needs to know each customer's total before it can bucket them.</p><p><code>NTILE(4) OVER (ORDER BY total DESC, customer_id)</code> - the customer_id tiebreaker makes bucket assignment deterministic when totals are equal.</p>"}, {"id": 40, "topic": "NTILE and quantile segmentation", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Give each customer a percentile standing by spend, so we can say where a given customer sits in the distribution.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, total and pct_rank: each customer's rounded total spend and PERCENT_RANK by spend ascending, rounded to 4 decimals.", "hint": "PERCENT_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY total).", "solution": "WITH s AS (SELECT customer_id, SUM(amount) AS total FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id) SELECT customer_id, ROUND(total,2) AS total, ROUND(PERCENT_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY total),4) AS pct_rank FROM s;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p><code>PERCENT_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY total)</code> gives a 0-to-1 relative standing - lower total means a lower percentile, so order ascending here rather than descending.</p>"}, {"id": 41, "topic": "NTILE and quantile segmentation", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Marketing wants to target our highest-value fifth. Pull just the top spend quintile of customers.", "prompt": "Return customer_id and total for the top spend quintile only (highest fifth of spenders), rounded to 2 decimals. Use NTILE(5).", "hint": "Compute quintiles in a CTE, then keep quintile 1.", "solution": "WITH s AS (SELECT customer_id, SUM(amount) AS total FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id), q AS (SELECT customer_id, total, NTILE(5) OVER (ORDER BY total DESC, customer_id) AS quintile FROM s) SELECT customer_id, ROUND(total,2) AS total FROM q WHERE quintile=1;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Two steps: assign quintiles with NTILE(5) in one CTE, then filter to just quintile 1 in the outer query - you can't filter a window function's result in the same SELECT that computes it.</p>"}, {"id": 112, "topic": "NTILE and quantile segmentation", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Marketing wants a finer-grained spend segmentation than quartiles this time: deciles.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, total and decile: each customer's rounded total spend and spend decile using NTILE(10), highest spend first.", "hint": "NTILE(10) splits ordered rows into 10 roughly equal buckets.", "solution": "WITH s AS (SELECT customer_id, SUM(amount) AS total FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id) SELECT customer_id, ROUND(total,2) AS total, NTILE(10) OVER (ORDER BY total DESC, customer_id) AS decile FROM s;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Identical shape to the quartile question, just <code>NTILE(10)</code> instead of NTILE(4) - the number in NTILE is simply how many buckets to split into.</p>"}, {"id": 113, "topic": "NTILE and quantile segmentation", "diff": "hard", "ordered": true, "ask": "Summarize the quartiles themselves: what's the average spend within each quartile?", "prompt": "Using a CTE with NTILE(4), return quartile and avg_spend: the average total spend within each spend quartile, ordered by quartile.", "hint": "Compute the quartile in a CTE first, then GROUP BY it in the outer query.", "solution": "WITH s AS (SELECT customer_id, SUM(amount) AS total FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id), q AS (SELECT customer_id, total, NTILE(4) OVER (ORDER BY total DESC, customer_id) AS quartile FROM s) SELECT quartile, ROUND(AVG(total),2) AS avg_spend FROM q GROUP BY quartile ORDER BY quartile;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Two layers: assign the quartile with NTILE in a CTE, then GROUP BY that quartile column in the outer query to summarize it.</p><p>You can't GROUP BY a window function's own output in the same SELECT it's computed in - that's exactly why the CTE exists here.</p>"}, {"id": 114, "topic": "NTILE and quantile segmentation", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Who are our lowest-spending fifth of customers, the ones most at risk of churn?", "prompt": "Using NTILE(5), return customer_id and total for customers in the bottom spend quintile.", "hint": "Order ascending this time so quintile 1 is the lowest, then keep the highest-numbered quintile.", "solution": "WITH s AS (SELECT customer_id, SUM(amount) AS total FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id), q AS (SELECT customer_id, total, NTILE(5) OVER (ORDER BY total DESC, customer_id) AS quintile FROM s) SELECT customer_id, ROUND(total,2) AS total FROM q WHERE quintile=5;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Same NTILE(5) setup as the top-quintile question, just keep the opposite end: <code>WHERE quintile = 5</code> instead of quintile = 1.</p>"}, {"id": 115, "topic": "NTILE and quantile segmentation", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "This time, rank customers from lowest to highest spend so quartile 1 means the smallest spenders.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, total and quartile using NTILE(4) ordered so quartile 1 is the lowest spenders.", "hint": "Order the window ascending instead of descending.", "solution": "WITH s AS (SELECT customer_id, SUM(amount) AS total FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id) SELECT customer_id, ROUND(total,2) AS total, NTILE(4) OVER (ORDER BY total ASC, customer_id) AS quartile FROM s;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Only one thing changes from the standard quartile pattern: order the window ascending (<code>ORDER BY total ASC</code>) instead of descending, so quartile 1 becomes the smallest spenders instead of the largest.</p>"}, {"topic": "Date and time logic", "diff": "easy", "ask": "Add a clean year-month label to the first 10 transactions.", "prompt": "Return transaction_id and mth (year-month of txn_date as text like 2024-05) for the first 10 transactions by transaction_id.", "hint": "strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date).", "solution": "SELECT transaction_id, strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date) AS mth FROM transactions ORDER BY transaction_id LIMIT 10;", "ordered": false, "id": 164, "bonus": false}, {"topic": "Date and time logic", "diff": "medium", "ask": "For customers who signed up via the Paid channel, show how many days after signup their FIRST transaction happened.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, acquisition_channel and days_to_first_txn (days between signup_date and the customer's earliest txn_date) for customers acquired via the Paid channel.", "hint": "Join customers to transactions, filter to acquisition_channel='Paid', then MIN(txn_date) minus signup_date via julianday().", "solution": "SELECT c.customer_id, c.acquisition_channel, CAST(julianday(MIN(t.txn_date)) - julianday(c.signup_date) AS INT) AS days_to_first_txn FROM customers c JOIN transactions t ON t.customer_id=c.customer_id WHERE c.acquisition_channel='Paid' GROUP BY c.customer_id, c.acquisition_channel, c.signup_date ORDER BY c.customer_id;", "ordered": false, "id": 165, "bonus": false, "clues": "<p>Join customers to transactions, filtered to the Paid channel, before doing any date math.</p><p><code>MIN(t.txn_date)</code> per customer gives their first transaction; subtract <code>julianday(signup_date)</code> from it and cast to INT for a whole day count.</p>"}, {"topic": "Date and time logic", "diff": "hard", "ask": "For each signup month, compute the AVERAGE days-to-first-transaction across that month's customers, and flag any month averaging more than 14 days as 'slow onboarding'. Product wants to know if onboarding speed has been drifting.", "prompt": "Return signup_month, avg_days_to_first and a flag column ('slow onboarding' if the average exceeds 14, else 'on track'), grouped by signup month and ordered by signup month.", "hint": "First find each customer's earliest transaction date in a CTE, join to their signup_date, then GROUP BY signup month with a CASE on the AVG.", "solution": "WITH first_txn AS (SELECT customer_id, MIN(txn_date) AS first_txn_date FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id), joined AS (SELECT c.customer_id, strftime('%Y-%m', c.signup_date) AS signup_month, julianday(f.first_txn_date) - julianday(c.signup_date) AS days_to_first FROM customers c JOIN first_txn f ON f.customer_id=c.customer_id) SELECT signup_month, ROUND(AVG(days_to_first),1) AS avg_days_to_first, CASE WHEN AVG(days_to_first) > 14 THEN 'slow onboarding' ELSE 'on track' END AS flag FROM joined GROUP BY signup_month ORDER BY signup_month;", "ordered": true, "id": 166, "bonus": false, "clues": "<p>\"Each customer's first transaction\" means <code>MIN(txn_date)</code> grouped by customer_id - build that as its own CTE before touching signup dates.</p><p>Join that CTE back to customers to get each customer's signup_date and signup month, then compute the day gap with <code>julianday(...) - julianday(...)</code> per customer.</p><p>Only after that per-customer gap exists can you GROUP BY signup month and AVG it - the CASE flag on 'slow onboarding' goes on that same AVG in the outer, grouped query.</p>"}, {"id": 196, "topic": "Date and time logic", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should cover extracting parts of a date/timestamp (year, month, day, day-of-week), computing differences between two dates, and truncating a timestamp down to a coarser grain (e.g. rounding down to the start of the month or week) for grouping purposes. It should mention date arithmetic (adding/subtracting an interval) and the common pattern of bucketing raw timestamps into a coarser period (like month) specifically so they can be used cleanly in a GROUP BY."}, {"id": 42, "topic": "Date and time logic", "diff": "easy", "ordered": false, "ask": "I want to group by month shortly. First, add a clean year-month label to each transaction.", "prompt": "Return transaction_id and mth: the year-month of each transaction as text like 2024-05.", "hint": "strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date).", "solution": "SELECT transaction_id, strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date) AS mth FROM transactions;", "bonus": true}, {"id": 43, "topic": "Date and time logic", "diff": "medium", "ordered": true, "ask": "Give me the monthly revenue series, in order, so I can drop it straight into a chart.", "prompt": "Return mth and revenue: total revenue per calendar month rounded to 2 decimals, ordered by month.", "hint": "Group by the truncated month.", "solution": "SELECT strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date) AS mth, ROUND(SUM(amount),2) AS revenue FROM transactions GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Group by the truncated month (<code>strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date)</code>), then ORDER BY that same column so the series reads chronologically.</p>"}, {"id": 44, "topic": "Date and time logic", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "For an activation-speed metric, how many days pass between signup and a customer's first transaction?", "prompt": "Return customer_id and days_to_first: the whole number of days from each customer's signup to their first transaction, for customers who transacted.", "hint": "MIN(txn_date) per customer, then julianday difference.", "solution": "WITH f AS (SELECT customer_id, MIN(txn_date) AS first_txn FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id) SELECT c.customer_id, CAST(julianday(f.first_txn) - julianday(c.signup_date) AS INT) AS days_to_first FROM customers c JOIN f ON c.customer_id=f.customer_id;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Find each customer's earliest transaction in a CTE first (<code>MIN(txn_date)</code> grouped by customer_id).</p><p>Join that back to customers to get signup_date, then <code>julianday(first_txn) - julianday(signup_date)</code>, cast to INT.</p>"}, {"id": 116, "topic": "Date and time logic", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Does signup day of week matter? Tag each customer with the numeric weekday they joined on.", "prompt": "Return customer_id and signup_weekday: the numeric day of week of signup_date (0 = Sunday).", "hint": "strftime('%w', col) returns a weekday number as text; cast it to an integer.", "solution": "SELECT customer_id, CAST(strftime('%w', signup_date) AS INT) AS signup_weekday FROM customers;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p><code>strftime('%w', date)</code> returns the weekday as text (0=Sunday through 6=Saturday) - cast it to an integer with <code>CAST(... AS INT)</code> if you want it as a number rather than text.</p>"}, {"id": 117, "topic": "Date and time logic", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "For every transaction, how many days after that customer's signup did it happen?", "prompt": "Return transaction_id and days_since_signup: whole days between the customer's signup and that transaction.", "hint": "Join to customers to get signup_date, then use julianday() to difference the two dates.", "solution": "SELECT t.transaction_id, CAST(julianday(t.txn_date) - julianday(c.signup_date) AS INT) AS days_since_signup FROM transactions t JOIN customers c ON t.customer_id=c.customer_id;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Join transactions to customers to bring signup_date onto each transaction row.</p><p><code>julianday(txn_date) - julianday(signup_date)</code>, cast to INT, gives the day gap for every single transaction - no grouping needed.</p>"}, {"id": 118, "topic": "Date and time logic", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Which customers joined during the second quarter of 2024?", "prompt": "Return customer_id for customers who signed up between 2024-04-01 and 2024-06-30.", "hint": "BETWEEN works directly on the YYYY-MM-DD text dates.", "solution": "SELECT customer_id FROM customers WHERE signup_date BETWEEN '2024-04-01' AND '2024-06-30';", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p><code>BETWEEN</code> works directly on the YYYY-MM-DD text dates since that format sorts and compares correctly as text.</p><p>Q2 2024 runs from April 1st through June 30th - both boundaries are inclusive with BETWEEN.</p>"}, {"id": 119, "topic": "Date and time logic", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Which months were unusually busy, with more than 15 transactions?", "prompt": "Return mth and txn_count: months with more than 15 transactions.", "hint": "Group by the truncated month, then filter the aggregate with HAVING.", "solution": "SELECT strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date) AS mth, COUNT(*) AS txn_count FROM transactions GROUP BY mth HAVING COUNT(*) > 15;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Group by the truncated month, COUNT(*) per month, then filter that count with HAVING (not WHERE, since it's filtering an aggregate).</p>"}, {"topic": "Cohort retention", "diff": "easy", "ask": "Assign each of the first 10 customers (by customer_id) to their signup-month cohort.", "prompt": "Return customer_id and cohort_month (year-month of signup_date) for the first 10 customers by customer_id.", "hint": "strftime('%Y-%m', signup_date).", "solution": "SELECT customer_id, strftime('%Y-%m', signup_date) AS cohort_month FROM customers ORDER BY customer_id LIMIT 10;", "ordered": false, "id": 167, "bonus": false}, {"topic": "Cohort retention", "diff": "medium", "ask": "For Enterprise customers only, size each signup cohort - how many Enterprise customers joined per signup month?", "prompt": "Return cohort_month and cohort_size (count of Enterprise customers) grouped by signup month, ordered by signup month.", "hint": "Filter to segment='Enterprise' before grouping by the signup month.", "solution": "SELECT strftime('%Y-%m', signup_date) AS cohort_month, COUNT(*) AS cohort_size FROM customers WHERE segment='Enterprise' GROUP BY cohort_month ORDER BY cohort_month;", "ordered": true, "id": 168, "bonus": false, "clues": "<p>Filter to <code>segment='Enterprise'</code> in the WHERE clause before grouping - the filter has to apply to the raw customers, not after the cohort is already sized.</p><p>Group by <code>strftime('%Y-%m', signup_date)</code> exactly like the unfiltered cohort-sizing pattern.</p>"}, {"topic": "Cohort retention", "diff": "hard", "ask": "For each signup cohort AND acquisition channel, what share of that specific slice came back the very next month? Leadership wants retention broken down by channel, not just overall.", "prompt": "Return cohort_month, acquisition_channel, cohort_size and retention_m1 (share of the cohort transacting again the month right after signup), grouped by cohort month and channel, ordered by cohort month then channel.", "hint": "Define the cohort with its 'next month' label and channel in a CTE, LEFT JOIN to distinct monthly activity, then GROUP BY cohort month and channel.", "solution": "WITH cohort AS (SELECT customer_id, strftime('%Y-%m', signup_date) AS cohort_month, strftime('%Y-%m', date(signup_date,'start of month','+1 month')) AS next_m, acquisition_channel FROM customers), act AS (SELECT DISTINCT customer_id, strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date) AS tm FROM transactions) SELECT c.cohort_month, c.acquisition_channel, COUNT(DISTINCT c.customer_id) AS cohort_size, ROUND(COUNT(DISTINCT a.customer_id)*1.0/COUNT(DISTINCT c.customer_id),2) AS retention_m1 FROM cohort c LEFT JOIN act a ON a.customer_id=c.customer_id AND a.tm=c.next_m GROUP BY c.cohort_month, c.acquisition_channel ORDER BY c.cohort_month, c.acquisition_channel;", "ordered": true, "id": 169, "bonus": false, "clues": "<p>This is the standard cohort-retention shape with one extra grouping column (channel) added throughout - don't drop it partway through the query.</p><p>Define the cohort CTE with customer_id, the cohort month, the 'month after signup' label (<code>date(signup_date,'start of month','+1 month')</code>), and acquisition_channel, all four columns together.</p><p>LEFT JOIN (not INNER) from the cohort to a distinct-activity CTE, matching on customer_id and the 'month after' label - customers with no matching activity must still appear as a row.</p><p>GROUP BY both cohort month and channel in the final SELECT, since the ask is retention per cohort <i>per channel</i>, not retention per cohort overall.</p>"}, {"id": 197, "topic": "Cohort retention", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should explain the standard cohort-retention pattern: assigning each customer to a cohort based on their first activity (e.g. signup month), then measuring, for each cohort, what fraction of its original members were still active in each subsequent period. It should mention that this typically requires a self-join or a window function to compare a customer's first-activity date against their activity in later periods, and that the output is usually shaped as a cohort-by-period grid (rows = cohorts, columns = periods since first activity, cells = retention rate)."}, {"id": 45, "topic": "Cohort retention", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "We think about customers by when they joined. Assign each customer to their signup-month cohort.", "prompt": "Return customer_id and cohort_month: each customer's signup cohort as year-month text.", "hint": "strftime on signup_date.", "solution": "SELECT customer_id, strftime('%Y-%m', signup_date) AS cohort_month FROM customers;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p><code>strftime('%Y-%m', signup_date)</code> is the cohort label - no grouping or aggregation needed here, just a per-customer tag.</p>"}, {"id": 46, "topic": "Cohort retention", "diff": "medium", "ordered": true, "ask": "How big is each signup cohort? I want the number of customers per joining month.", "prompt": "Return cohort_month and cohort_size: the number of customers in each signup-month cohort, ordered by month.", "hint": "Group customers by signup month.", "solution": "SELECT strftime('%Y-%m', signup_date) AS cohort_month, COUNT(*) AS cohort_size FROM customers GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Group customers by their signup month and COUNT(*) - that's the whole pattern, no JOIN needed since everything lives on the customers table.</p>"}, {"id": 47, "topic": "Cohort retention", "diff": "hard", "ordered": true, "ask": "This is the one leadership cares about. For each signup cohort, what share came back and transacted in the month right after they joined?", "prompt": "Return cohort_month, cohort_size, retained_m1 and retention_m1: for each signup cohort, its size, how many customers transacted in the month immediately after signup, and that retention rate rounded to 2 decimals. Order by cohort_month.", "hint": "Define next month per cohort, left join to distinct monthly activity, divide.", "solution": "WITH cohort AS (SELECT customer_id, strftime('%Y-%m', signup_date) AS cm, strftime('%Y-%m', date(signup_date,'start of month','+1 month')) AS next_m FROM customers), act AS (SELECT DISTINCT customer_id, strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date) AS tm FROM transactions) SELECT c.cm AS cohort_month, COUNT(DISTINCT c.customer_id) AS cohort_size, COUNT(DISTINCT a.customer_id) AS retained_m1, ROUND(COUNT(DISTINCT a.customer_id)*1.0/COUNT(DISTINCT c.customer_id),2) AS retention_m1 FROM cohort c LEFT JOIN act a ON a.customer_id=c.customer_id AND a.tm=c.next_m GROUP BY c.cm ORDER BY c.cm;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Three pieces: a cohort CTE (customer_id, their signup month, and the month right after signup via <code>date(signup_date,'start of month','+1 month')</code>), an activity CTE (distinct customer_id + month from transactions), and a LEFT JOIN between them matching on customer and that \"next month\" label.</p><p>LEFT JOIN is essential - customers who never came back still need to appear as a row (with NULL activity) so they count in the denominator.</p>"}, {"id": 120, "topic": "Cohort retention", "diff": "hard", "ordered": true, "ask": "Retention often keeps dropping. Check the SECOND month after signup, not just the first.", "prompt": "Return cohort_month and m2_retention: the retention rate into the second month after signup, ordered by cohort_month.", "hint": "Shift the comparison month forward by two months instead of one.", "solution": "WITH cohort AS (SELECT customer_id, strftime('%Y-%m', signup_date) AS cm, strftime('%Y-%m', date(signup_date,'start of month','+2 month')) AS m2 FROM customers), act AS (SELECT DISTINCT customer_id, strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date) AS tm FROM transactions) SELECT c.cm AS cohort_month, ROUND(COUNT(DISTINCT a.customer_id)*1.0/COUNT(DISTINCT c.customer_id),2) AS m2_retention FROM cohort c LEFT JOIN act a ON a.customer_id=c.customer_id AND a.tm=c.m2 GROUP BY c.cm ORDER BY c.cm;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Identical structure to the month-1 retention question - only the date-math changes, from <code>'+1 month'</code> to <code>'+2 month'</code> when building the comparison month.</p>"}, {"id": 121, "topic": "Cohort retention", "diff": "hard", "ordered": true, "ask": "Forget transacting for a moment - what share of each cohort ever reached the activated stage at all?", "prompt": "Return cohort_month and pct_activated: the percent of each signup cohort that ever reached the activated event, ordered by cohort_month.", "hint": "Use the events table instead of transactions for the activation check.", "solution": "WITH cohort AS (SELECT customer_id, strftime('%Y-%m', signup_date) AS cm FROM customers), act AS (SELECT DISTINCT customer_id FROM events WHERE event_type='activated') SELECT c.cm AS cohort_month, ROUND(COUNT(DISTINCT a.customer_id)*1.0/COUNT(DISTINCT c.customer_id),2) AS pct_activated FROM cohort c LEFT JOIN act a ON a.customer_id=c.customer_id GROUP BY c.cm ORDER BY c.cm;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Same cohort-then-LEFT-JOIN shape, but the \"activity\" side now comes from events filtered to <code>event_type='activated'</code>, not from transactions at all.</p>"}, {"id": 122, "topic": "Cohort retention", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Out of all our signup cohorts, which single one had the best first-month retention?", "prompt": "Return cohort_month and retention_m1 for the single cohort with the highest month-1 retention rate.", "hint": "Compute retention per cohort first, then keep only the top row.", "solution": "WITH cohort AS (SELECT customer_id, strftime('%Y-%m', signup_date) AS cm, strftime('%Y-%m', date(signup_date,'start of month','+1 month')) AS next_m FROM customers), act AS (SELECT DISTINCT customer_id, strftime('%Y-%m', txn_date) AS tm FROM transactions), r AS (SELECT c.cm AS cohort_month, COUNT(DISTINCT a.customer_id)*1.0/COUNT(DISTINCT c.customer_id) AS retention_m1 FROM cohort c LEFT JOIN act a ON a.customer_id=c.customer_id AND a.tm=c.next_m GROUP BY c.cm) SELECT cohort_month, ROUND(retention_m1,2) AS retention_m1 FROM r ORDER BY retention_m1 DESC LIMIT 1;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Compute retention per cohort exactly like the standard retention query, wrapped as a CTE - then in the outer query, ORDER BY retention descending and LIMIT 1 to keep just the best one.</p>"}, {"id": 123, "topic": "Cohort retention", "diff": "hard", "ordered": true, "ask": "Beyond just returning, what share of each cohort became a genuine repeat payer?", "prompt": "Return cohort_month and repeat_rate: the percent of each signup cohort that ever reached repeat_payment, ordered by cohort_month.", "hint": "Same pattern as activation rate, just check for the repeat_payment event instead.", "solution": "WITH cohort AS (SELECT customer_id, strftime('%Y-%m', signup_date) AS cm FROM customers), act AS (SELECT DISTINCT customer_id FROM events WHERE event_type='repeat_payment') SELECT c.cm AS cohort_month, ROUND(COUNT(DISTINCT a.customer_id)*1.0/COUNT(DISTINCT c.customer_id),2) AS repeat_rate FROM cohort c LEFT JOIN act a ON a.customer_id=c.customer_id GROUP BY c.cm ORDER BY c.cm;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Same activation-rate shape as \"share who ever activated,\" just point the activity CTE at <code>event_type='repeat_payment'</code> instead.</p>"}, {"topic": "Funnel conversion and rate math", "diff": "easy", "ask": "Walk me through our funnel. How many distinct customers reached each stage: signup, activated, first_payment?", "prompt": "Return signups, activated and paid: distinct-customer counts reaching each of those event types, as one row.", "hint": "COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event_type = '...' THEN customer_id END) for each stage.", "solution": "SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event_type='signup' THEN customer_id END) AS signups, COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event_type='activated' THEN customer_id END) AS activated, COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event_type='first_payment' THEN customer_id END) AS paid FROM events;", "ordered": false, "id": 170, "bonus": false}, {"topic": "Funnel conversion and rate math", "diff": "medium", "ask": "Break the signup-to-activation rate down by customer segment. Which segment activates best?", "prompt": "Return segment, signups, activated and activation_rate (activated divided by signups, guarded against zero) grouped by segment, ordered by segment.", "hint": "Join events to customers for the segment, then conditional-aggregate signups and activated per segment.", "solution": "SELECT c.segment, COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN e.event_type='signup' THEN e.customer_id END) AS signups, COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN e.event_type='activated' THEN e.customer_id END) AS activated, ROUND(COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN e.event_type='activated' THEN e.customer_id END)*1.0/NULLIF(COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN e.event_type='signup' THEN e.customer_id END),0),3) AS activation_rate FROM events e JOIN customers c ON e.customer_id=c.customer_id GROUP BY c.segment ORDER BY c.segment;", "ordered": false, "id": 171, "bonus": false, "clues": "<p>Join events to customers to bring segment onto each event row, before doing any conditional counting.</p><p><code>COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event_type='signup' THEN customer_id END)</code> and the same pattern for 'activated', both grouped by segment - then divide one by the other with a NULLIF guard.</p>"}, {"topic": "Funnel conversion and rate math", "diff": "hard", "ask": "Across acquisition channels, compute signup-to-payment conversion, then rank the channels from best to worst converting. Marketing wants to double down on whichever channel converts best.", "prompt": "Return channel, signups, paid, conversion_rate and channel_rank (RANK() ordered by conversion_rate descending), ordered by channel_rank.", "hint": "Build per-channel signup/paid counts and the conversion rate in a CTE, then RANK() OVER (ORDER BY conversion_rate DESC) in the outer query.", "solution": "WITH by_channel AS (SELECT c.acquisition_channel AS channel, COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN e.event_type='signup' THEN e.customer_id END) AS signups, COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN e.event_type='first_payment' THEN e.customer_id END) AS paid FROM events e JOIN customers c ON e.customer_id=c.customer_id GROUP BY c.acquisition_channel) SELECT channel, signups, paid, ROUND(paid*1.0/NULLIF(signups,0),3) AS conversion_rate, RANK() OVER (ORDER BY paid*1.0/NULLIF(signups,0) DESC) AS channel_rank FROM by_channel ORDER BY channel_rank;", "ordered": false, "id": 172, "bonus": false, "clues": "<p>Build the per-channel signup/paid counts and the conversion rate first, in a CTE - conditional COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN ...) exactly like the funnel lesson, just GROUP BY channel instead of nothing.</p><p>Guard the rate with <code>NULLIF(signups, 0)</code> so a channel with zero signups doesn't crash the division.</p><p>RANK() the channels in the outer query, reading from that CTE: <code>RANK() OVER (ORDER BY conversion_rate DESC)</code> - you need the rate already computed before you can rank on it.</p>"}, {"id": 198, "topic": "Funnel conversion and rate math", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should explain that a funnel measures how many entities (customers, sessions) progress through a sequence of ordered steps, and that funnel conversion rate at each step is computed relative to a consistent denominator - most commonly the number who reached the very first step, though step-over-step conversion (each step relative to the step immediately before it) is a different, equally valid framing that tells a different story. It should mention the common implementation pattern of a conditional aggregate (SUM(CASE WHEN reached_step THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)) per step, or self-joins/window functions when step order and timing need to be strictly enforced."}, {"id": 48, "topic": "Funnel conversion and rate math", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Walk me through our funnel. How many distinct customers reached each stage?", "prompt": "Return event_type and customers: the number of distinct customers who reached each funnel stage.", "hint": "Group events by type, count distinct customers.", "solution": "SELECT event_type, COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id) AS customers FROM events GROUP BY event_type;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>No CASE needed for this one - just GROUP BY event_type directly and COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id) per stage.</p>"}, {"id": 49, "topic": "Funnel conversion and rate math", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Put the whole funnel on one row: signups, activated, first payment, and repeat payment, side by side.", "prompt": "In a single row return signups, activated, first_payment and repeat_payment: distinct customers at each stage, using conditional aggregation.", "hint": "COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event_type=... THEN customer_id END).", "solution": "SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event_type='signup' THEN customer_id END) AS signups, COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event_type='activated' THEN customer_id END) AS activated, COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event_type='first_payment' THEN customer_id END) AS first_payment, COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event_type='repeat_payment' THEN customer_id END) AS repeat_payment FROM events;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Putting several stages \"side by side\" (as columns, not rows) is what conditional aggregation is for: one <code>COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event_type='...' THEN customer_id END)</code> per stage, all in the same SELECT, no GROUP BY.</p>"}, {"id": 50, "topic": "Funnel conversion and rate math", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "What is our activation rate, activated over signups? And make sure it does not break if signups were ever zero.", "prompt": "Return activation_rate: distinct activated customers divided by distinct signups, rounded to 3 decimals, protected against divide-by-zero.", "hint": "Use 1.0 multiplier and NULLIF on the denominator.", "solution": "SELECT ROUND(COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event_type='activated' THEN customer_id END) * 1.0 / NULLIF(COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event_type='signup' THEN customer_id END),0),3) AS activation_rate FROM events;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Two conditional counts divided by each other: activated over signup.</p><p>Multiply by <code>1.0</code> so the division doesn't truncate to an integer, and wrap the denominator in <code>NULLIF(..., 0)</code> so a zero-signup edge case returns NULL instead of crashing.</p>"}, {"id": 124, "topic": "Funnel conversion and rate math", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Of the customers we activated, what fraction actually went on to pay?", "prompt": "Return activation_to_payment_rate: distinct first_payment customers divided by distinct activated customers, rounded to 3 decimals.", "hint": "Use conditional COUNT(DISTINCT ...) for each stage, then divide.", "solution": "SELECT ROUND(COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event_type='first_payment' THEN customer_id END) * 1.0 / NULLIF(COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event_type='activated' THEN customer_id END),0),3) AS activation_to_payment_rate FROM events;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Same conditional-count-and-divide shape as activation rate, just move one stage further down the funnel: first_payment over activated.</p>"}, {"id": 125, "topic": "Funnel conversion and rate math", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Of everyone who paid once, what share came back to pay again?", "prompt": "Return repeat_rate: distinct repeat_payment customers divided by distinct first_payment customers, rounded to 3 decimals.", "hint": "Same conditional-count-and-divide pattern, one stage further down the funnel.", "solution": "SELECT ROUND(COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event_type='repeat_payment' THEN customer_id END) * 1.0 / NULLIF(COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event_type='first_payment' THEN customer_id END),0),3) AS repeat_rate FROM events;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Same pattern again, one more stage down: repeat_payment over first_payment.</p>"}, {"id": 126, "topic": "Funnel conversion and rate math", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "For each stage past signup, how many signed-up customers never made it that far?", "prompt": "Return event_type and drop_off: for activated, first_payment and repeat_payment, the number of signed-up customers who never reached that stage.", "hint": "Subtract each stage's distinct customer count from the total distinct signups, stacking the three stages with UNION ALL.", "solution": "SELECT 'activated' AS event_type, (SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id) FROM events WHERE event_type='signup') - COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id) AS drop_off FROM events WHERE event_type='activated' UNION ALL SELECT 'first_payment', (SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id) FROM events WHERE event_type='signup') - COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id) FROM events WHERE event_type='first_payment' UNION ALL SELECT 'repeat_payment', (SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id) FROM events WHERE event_type='signup') - COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id) FROM events WHERE event_type='repeat_payment';", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>\"Never made it that far\" is signup count minus that stage's count - compute the signup total once as its own scalar subquery, then subtract each stage's own COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id) from it.</p><p>Stack the three stages (activated, first_payment, repeat_payment) into one result with UNION ALL, each block doing the same subtraction.</p>"}, {"id": 127, "topic": "Funnel conversion and rate math", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Give leadership every stage-to-stage conversion rate in one summary row.", "prompt": "In a single row, return signup_to_activated, activated_to_first_payment and first_payment_to_repeat, each rounded to 3 decimals.", "hint": "Three separate conditional-count ratios, computed side by side in one SELECT.", "solution": "SELECT ROUND(COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event_type='activated' THEN customer_id END)*1.0/NULLIF(COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event_type='signup' THEN customer_id END),0),3) AS signup_to_activated, ROUND(COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event_type='first_payment' THEN customer_id END)*1.0/NULLIF(COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event_type='activated' THEN customer_id END),0),3) AS activated_to_first_payment, ROUND(COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event_type='repeat_payment' THEN customer_id END)*1.0/NULLIF(COUNT(DISTINCT CASE WHEN event_type='first_payment' THEN customer_id END),0),3) AS first_payment_to_repeat FROM events;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Three separate conditional-count ratios, computed in the same SELECT rather than stacked as rows: signup-to-activated, activated-to-payment, payment-to-repeat, each its own column with its own NULLIF guard.</p>"}, {"topic": "Cross-product attachment", "diff": "easy", "ask": "For an attachment view, show the top 10 customers by how many distinct products they've used.", "prompt": "Return customer_id and distinct_products (COUNT(DISTINCT product_id)), top 10 rows ordered by distinct_products descending then customer_id ascending as a tiebreaker.", "hint": "COUNT(DISTINCT product_id) GROUP BY customer_id, with customer_id as a tiebreaker in the ORDER BY.", "solution": "SELECT customer_id, COUNT(DISTINCT product_id) AS distinct_products FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id ORDER BY distinct_products DESC, customer_id LIMIT 10;", "ordered": false, "id": 173, "bonus": false}, {"topic": "Cross-product attachment", "diff": "medium", "ask": "Compare average product attachment between segments - which segment engages with the broadest range of products?", "prompt": "Return segment and avg_attachment (average distinct-product count per customer within that segment), ordered by avg_attachment descending.", "hint": "Build per-customer distinct-product counts with a segment column in a CTE, then AVG that grouped by segment.", "solution": "WITH attach AS (SELECT c.customer_id, c.segment, COUNT(DISTINCT t.product_id) AS distinct_products FROM transactions t JOIN customers c ON t.customer_id=c.customer_id GROUP BY c.customer_id, c.segment) SELECT segment, ROUND(AVG(distinct_products),2) AS avg_attachment FROM attach GROUP BY segment ORDER BY avg_attachment DESC;", "ordered": false, "id": 174, "bonus": false, "clues": "<p>Build each customer's distinct-product count with their segment attached in a CTE (JOIN + GROUP BY).</p><p>Then AVG that count, GROUP BY segment, in the outer query - two clearly separate steps, not one combined GROUP BY.</p>"}, {"topic": "Cross-product attachment", "diff": "hard", "ask": "Flag each customer as 'single-product' (attachment = 1), 'multi-product' (2-3), or 'power user' (4+), then show how many customers fall in each tier along with their combined revenue.", "prompt": "Return tier, customers (count) and total_revenue for each attachment tier, ordered by customers descending.", "hint": "Compute distinct-product count and total spend per customer in a CTE, CASE that into a tier in a second CTE, then GROUP BY tier.", "solution": "WITH attach AS (SELECT customer_id, COUNT(DISTINCT product_id) AS distinct_products, SUM(amount) AS total_spend FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id), tiered AS (SELECT customer_id, distinct_products, total_spend, CASE WHEN distinct_products=1 THEN 'single-product' WHEN distinct_products BETWEEN 2 AND 3 THEN 'multi-product' ELSE 'power user' END AS tier FROM attach) SELECT tier, COUNT(*) AS customers, ROUND(SUM(total_spend),2) AS total_revenue FROM tiered GROUP BY tier ORDER BY customers DESC;", "ordered": false, "id": 175, "bonus": false, "clues": "<p>Three layers here, each building on the last: raw per-customer metrics, then a tier label, then a group-level summary.</p><p>Layer one (CTE): each customer's distinct product count and total spend.</p><p>Layer two (a second CTE reading from the first): CASE that distinct-product count into 'single-product' / 'multi-product' / 'power user'.</p><p>Layer three (the outer query): GROUP BY the tier column from layer two, COUNT(*) and SUM(total_spend).</p>"}, {"id": 199, "topic": "Cross-product attachment", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should explain that a CROSS JOIN produces every possible combination of rows between two tables (the Cartesian product), with no join condition at all, and that this is genuinely useful - not just an accidental mistake - when you deliberately need every combination, such as pairing every customer with every possible period to make sure periods with zero activity still appear in a report rather than being silently missing. It should mention the risk of an accidental cross join (a join with a missing or malformed ON condition) silently exploding row counts."}, {"id": 51, "topic": "Cross-product attachment", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "For an attachment view, how many distinct products does each customer use?", "prompt": "Return customer_id and product_count: the number of distinct products each customer has transacted in.", "hint": "COUNT(DISTINCT product_id) per customer.", "solution": "SELECT customer_id, COUNT(DISTINCT product_id) AS product_count FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p><code>COUNT(DISTINCT product_id)</code> grouped by customer_id - the DISTINCT is what stops repeat purchases of the same product from inflating the count.</p>"}, {"id": 52, "topic": "Cross-product attachment", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Who are our most engaged customers by breadth? Show the ones using three or more products.", "prompt": "Return customer_id and product_count for customers who use 3 or more distinct products.", "hint": "HAVING on the distinct count.", "solution": "SELECT customer_id, COUNT(DISTINCT product_id) AS product_count FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT product_id) >= 3;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Same GROUP BY/COUNT(DISTINCT) as basic attachment, with a HAVING clause on top to keep only customers at or above the threshold.</p>"}, {"id": 53, "topic": "Cross-product attachment", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Product wants to understand the Checkout to Credit relationship. Which customers use both?", "prompt": "Return customer_id for customers who use both Checkout (product_id 1) and Credit (product_id 3).", "hint": "Filter to those two products and require both are present.", "solution": "SELECT customer_id FROM transactions WHERE product_id IN (1,3) GROUP BY customer_id HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT product_id)=2;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Filter to just the two products of interest first (<code>WHERE product_id IN (1,3)</code>), then GROUP BY customer and require both are present: <code>HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT product_id) = 2</code>.</p><p>Filtering to only those two products before counting is what makes \"= 2\" mean \"has both,\" rather than needing to check for two specific values explicitly.</p>"}, {"id": 128, "topic": "Cross-product attachment", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Do we have any true power users who've transacted in every single product we sell?", "prompt": "Return customer_id for customers who used 4 or more distinct products.", "hint": "GROUP BY customer_id, then HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT product_id) at the threshold.", "solution": "SELECT customer_id FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT product_id) >= 4;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Same GROUP BY/HAVING attachment pattern, just set the HAVING threshold to the total number of products that exist (check the schema for how many that is).</p>"}, {"id": 129, "topic": "Cross-product attachment", "diff": "medium", "ordered": true, "ask": "Rank our products by how many distinct customers actually use them.", "prompt": "Return product_id and customer_count, ordered from most to fewest distinct customers.", "hint": "GROUP BY product_id, COUNT DISTINCT customers, then ORDER BY that count descending.", "solution": "SELECT product_id, COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id) AS customer_count FROM transactions GROUP BY product_id ORDER BY customer_count DESC;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>This flips attachment around: instead of products-per-customer, it's customers-per-product.</p><p>GROUP BY product_id, <code>COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id)</code>, then ORDER BY that count descending.</p>"}, {"id": 130, "topic": "Cross-product attachment", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Find Checkout users who have never touched our Credit product - a natural cross-sell audience.", "prompt": "Return the distinct customer_id values who used Checkout (product_id 1) but never used Credit (product_id 3).", "hint": "Use NOT IN with a subquery of Credit users.", "solution": "SELECT DISTINCT customer_id FROM transactions WHERE product_id=1 AND customer_id NOT IN (SELECT customer_id FROM transactions WHERE product_id=3);", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p><code>NOT IN (SELECT customer_id FROM transactions WHERE product_id=3)</code> excludes anyone who's ever touched Credit.</p><p>Combine that with a filter for Checkout users (<code>product_id=1</code>) in the outer query, and DISTINCT the result since a Checkout user could have several Checkout transactions.</p>"}, {"id": 131, "topic": "Cross-product attachment", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "As a single portfolio health metric, how many distinct products does the average transacting customer use?", "prompt": "Return avg_products: the average number of distinct products used per transacting customer, rounded to 2 decimals.", "hint": "Compute the per-customer product count in a CTE, then average that.", "solution": "WITH pc AS (SELECT customer_id, COUNT(DISTINCT product_id) AS n FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id) SELECT ROUND(AVG(n),2) AS avg_products FROM pc;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Build the per-customer product count in a CTE (same as basic attachment), then just AVG that count in the outer query - no GROUP BY needed since it's a single portfolio-wide number.</p>"}, {"topic": "Subtotals with ROLLUP", "diff": "easy", "ask": "Give me revenue by segment, and include a grand total row so the numbers foot to the whole business.", "prompt": "Return segment (or 'ALL' for the total row) and revenue, one row per segment plus one total row.", "hint": "GROUP BY segment, then UNION ALL a second query with no GROUP BY labelled 'ALL'.", "solution": "SELECT c.segment, ROUND(SUM(t.amount),2) AS revenue FROM transactions t JOIN customers c ON t.customer_id=c.customer_id GROUP BY c.segment UNION ALL SELECT 'ALL', ROUND(SUM(amount),2) FROM transactions;", "ordered": false, "id": 176, "bonus": false}, {"topic": "Subtotals with ROLLUP", "diff": "medium", "ask": "Same idea, but only for completed transactions: revenue by acquisition channel, plus an overall total row.", "prompt": "Return acquisition_channel (or 'ALL' for the total row) and revenue, for completed transactions only, one row per channel plus one total row.", "hint": "Filter both halves of the UNION ALL to status='completed'.", "solution": "SELECT c.acquisition_channel, ROUND(SUM(t.amount),2) AS revenue FROM transactions t JOIN customers c ON t.customer_id=c.customer_id WHERE t.status='completed' GROUP BY c.acquisition_channel UNION ALL SELECT 'ALL', ROUND(SUM(amount),2) FROM transactions WHERE status='completed';", "ordered": false, "id": 177, "bonus": false, "clues": "<p>Same channel-plus-total UNION ALL shape as the segment version - just add <code>WHERE status='completed'</code> to <i>both</i> halves of the UNION ALL, or the total row won't match the filtered detail rows.</p>"}, {"topic": "Subtotals with ROLLUP", "diff": "hard", "ask": "Break revenue down by segment AND status, with a subtotal per segment (across all statuses), plus one grand total row at the very bottom. Finance wants the full picture: the detail, the segment rollups, and the company total, in one result.", "prompt": "Return segment, status (or 'ALL STATUSES' for a segment subtotal, or 'ALL'/'ALL STATUSES' for the grand total) and revenue, covering the detail rows, one subtotal row per segment, and one grand total row.", "hint": "Three UNION ALL blocks: segment+status detail, segment-only subtotal labelled 'ALL STATUSES', and a single grand total labelled 'ALL'/'ALL STATUSES'.", "solution": "SELECT c.segment, t.status, ROUND(SUM(t.amount),2) AS revenue FROM transactions t JOIN customers c ON t.customer_id=c.customer_id GROUP BY c.segment, t.status UNION ALL SELECT c.segment, 'ALL STATUSES', ROUND(SUM(t.amount),2) FROM transactions t JOIN customers c ON t.customer_id=c.customer_id GROUP BY c.segment UNION ALL SELECT 'ALL', 'ALL STATUSES', ROUND(SUM(amount),2) FROM transactions;", "ordered": false, "id": 178, "bonus": false, "clues": "<p>Three UNION ALL blocks, and every block must return the exact same number of columns, in the same order, with compatible types.</p><p>Block one: the real detail, GROUP BY segment and status.</p><p>Block two: a subtotal per segment only - same query shape as block one, but GROUP BY segment alone, with a literal placeholder string standing in for status (something like <code>'ALL STATUSES'</code>).</p><p>Block three: the grand total - no GROUP BY, no JOIN needed, with literal placeholders in <i>both</i> the segment and status columns.</p>"}, {"id": 200, "topic": "Subtotals with ROLLUP", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should explain that ROLLUP extends GROUP BY to automatically add subtotal rows at each level of a grouping hierarchy, plus one grand-total row, without needing a manual UNION of separately-aggregated queries. It should mention that ROLLUP's grouping levels are hierarchical and order-sensitive (ROLLUP(a, b) produces subtotals for a-and-b, then a alone, then the grand total - not every possible combination), and that the subtotal rows are typically distinguishable because the columns being 'rolled up over' appear as NULL in those rows."}, {"id": 54, "topic": "Subtotals with ROLLUP", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Give me revenue by segment, and include a grand total row so the numbers foot to the whole.", "prompt": "Return segment and revenue: rounded total revenue per customer segment, plus a final row labelled 'ALL' with the grand total.", "hint": "Group query UNION ALL a total query.", "solution": "SELECT c.segment AS segment, ROUND(SUM(t.amount),2) AS revenue FROM transactions t JOIN customers c ON t.customer_id=c.customer_id GROUP BY c.segment UNION ALL SELECT 'ALL', ROUND(SUM(amount),2) FROM transactions;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>One grouped query (segment, SUM(amount)) UNION ALL a second query with no GROUP BY at all, labelled <code>'ALL'</code> in the segment column's position.</p><p>Both halves need the same number of columns, in the same order, for UNION ALL to work.</p>"}, {"id": 55, "topic": "Subtotals with ROLLUP", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Same idea for acquisition channel: revenue per channel, plus an overall total row.", "prompt": "Return acquisition_channel and revenue: rounded revenue per acquisition channel, plus an 'ALL' grand total row.", "hint": "Same UNION ALL subtotal pattern on channel.", "solution": "SELECT c.acquisition_channel AS acquisition_channel, ROUND(SUM(t.amount),2) AS revenue FROM transactions t JOIN customers c ON t.customer_id=c.customer_id GROUP BY c.acquisition_channel UNION ALL SELECT 'ALL', ROUND(SUM(amount),2) FROM transactions;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Identical shape to the segment-plus-total pattern - just change the GROUP BY column to acquisition_channel.</p>"}, {"id": 56, "topic": "Subtotals with ROLLUP", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "For a one-page summary, stack revenue by segment and revenue by country into a single labelled result.", "prompt": "Return dim, value and revenue: revenue grouped by segment and, stacked below, revenue grouped by country, each row labelled with which dimension it came from.", "hint": "Two grouped queries combined with UNION ALL and a literal dim label.", "solution": "SELECT 'segment' AS dim, c.segment AS value, ROUND(SUM(t.amount),2) AS revenue FROM transactions t JOIN customers c ON t.customer_id=c.customer_id GROUP BY c.segment UNION ALL SELECT 'country', c.country, ROUND(SUM(t.amount),2) FROM transactions t JOIN customers c ON t.customer_id=c.customer_id GROUP BY c.country;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Two separate grouped queries (one by segment, one by country), combined with UNION ALL - add a literal label column (e.g. <code>'segment'</code> / <code>'country'</code>) so the two breakdowns stay distinguishable once stacked.</p>"}, {"id": 132, "topic": "Subtotals with ROLLUP", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Executives want revenue broken down by country and segment, with a subtotal per country, and a grand total at the bottom.", "prompt": "Return dim1, dim2 and revenue: revenue by country and segment, plus a per-country 'ALL' subtotal row, plus a final 'ALL'/'ALL' grand total.", "hint": "Stack three UNION ALL levels: detail, country subtotal, and grand total.", "solution": "SELECT c.country AS dim1, c.segment AS dim2, ROUND(SUM(t.amount),2) AS revenue FROM transactions t JOIN customers c ON t.customer_id=c.customer_id GROUP BY c.country, c.segment UNION ALL SELECT c.country, 'ALL', ROUND(SUM(t.amount),2) FROM transactions t JOIN customers c ON t.customer_id=c.customer_id GROUP BY c.country UNION ALL SELECT 'ALL', 'ALL', ROUND(SUM(amount),2) FROM transactions;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Three UNION ALL blocks this time: the full country+segment detail, a country-only subtotal (GROUP BY country alone, segment column labelled <code>'ALL'</code>), and a single grand total (no GROUP BY, both label columns <code>'ALL'</code>).</p><p>All three blocks need the exact same column count and order.</p>"}, {"id": 133, "topic": "Subtotals with ROLLUP", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Give finance revenue by transaction status, plus the overall total at the bottom.", "prompt": "Return status and revenue per status, plus a final 'ALL' grand total row.", "hint": "UNION ALL a grand-total row onto the grouped result.", "solution": "SELECT status, ROUND(SUM(amount),2) AS revenue FROM transactions GROUP BY status UNION ALL SELECT 'ALL', ROUND(SUM(amount),2) FROM transactions;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Group by status, SUM(amount), then UNION ALL a totals-only row labelled <code>'ALL'</code> - same shape as the segment-plus-total pattern, just grouped by status instead.</p>"}, {"id": 134, "topic": "Subtotals with ROLLUP", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "The causal team wants revenue by experiment group, with a combined total row too.", "prompt": "Return exp_group and revenue per group, plus a final 'ALL' grand total row.", "hint": "Join transactions to campaign, group by exp_group, then UNION ALL a total row.", "solution": "SELECT g.exp_group, ROUND(SUM(t.amount),2) AS revenue FROM transactions t JOIN campaign g ON t.customer_id=g.customer_id GROUP BY g.exp_group UNION ALL SELECT 'ALL', ROUND(SUM(amount),2) FROM transactions;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Join transactions to campaign to get exp_group, GROUP BY that, then UNION ALL a no-GROUP-BY total row labelled <code>'ALL'</code>.</p>"}, {"id": 135, "topic": "Subtotals with ROLLUP", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Combine two different breakdowns, by segment and by channel, into one labelled summary, with a grand total underneath both.", "prompt": "Return dim, value and revenue: revenue by segment, stacked with revenue by acquisition_channel, plus a final 'ALL'/'ALL' grand total.", "hint": "Three UNION ALL blocks: segment breakdown, channel breakdown, then the total.", "solution": "SELECT 'segment' AS dim, c.segment AS value, ROUND(SUM(t.amount),2) AS revenue FROM transactions t JOIN customers c ON t.customer_id=c.customer_id GROUP BY c.segment UNION ALL SELECT 'channel', c.acquisition_channel, ROUND(SUM(t.amount),2) FROM transactions t JOIN customers c ON t.customer_id=c.customer_id GROUP BY c.acquisition_channel UNION ALL SELECT 'ALL', 'ALL', ROUND(SUM(amount),2) FROM transactions;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Three UNION ALL blocks: a segment breakdown, a channel breakdown (each with a literal label column so you can tell them apart), then one grand total block with both label columns set to <code>'ALL'</code>.</p>"}, {"topic": "Causal setup treatment and control", "diff": "easy", "ask": "We ran a campaign on the first of April 2024. Label the first 10 transactions (by transaction_id) as pre or post that date.", "prompt": "Return transaction_id, txn_date and period ('pre' if before 2024-04-01, else 'post'), for the first 10 transactions by transaction_id.", "hint": "CASE WHEN txn_date < '2024-04-01' THEN 'pre' ELSE 'post' END.", "solution": "SELECT transaction_id, txn_date, CASE WHEN txn_date < '2024-04-01' THEN 'pre' ELSE 'post' END AS period FROM transactions ORDER BY transaction_id LIMIT 10;", "ordered": false, "id": 179, "bonus": false}, {"topic": "Causal setup treatment and control", "diff": "medium", "ask": "Within Enterprise customers only, compare average transaction size for treatment vs control, before vs after the campaign date.", "prompt": "Return exp_group, period ('pre'/'post') and avg_amount, for Enterprise customers, grouped by group and period, ordered by group then period.", "hint": "Join transactions to both campaign (for exp_group) and customers (for segment), CASE for the period, then GROUP BY group and period.", "solution": "SELECT g.exp_group, CASE WHEN t.txn_date<'2024-04-01' THEN 'pre' ELSE 'post' END AS period, ROUND(AVG(t.amount),2) AS avg_amount FROM transactions t JOIN campaign g ON t.customer_id=g.customer_id JOIN customers c ON t.customer_id=c.customer_id WHERE c.segment='Enterprise' GROUP BY g.exp_group, period ORDER BY g.exp_group, period;", "ordered": false, "id": 180, "bonus": false, "clues": "<p>Join transactions to both <code>campaign</code> (for exp_group) and <code>customers</code> (for segment) - two separate JOINs, both needed.</p><p>Filter to Enterprise, CASE the date into pre/post, then GROUP BY both exp_group and that period column together.</p>"}, {"topic": "Causal setup treatment and control", "diff": "hard", "ask": "Go all the way: compute the actual difference-in-differences estimate - the treatment group's pre-to-post change in average transaction size, minus the control group's pre-to-post change - as a single number.", "prompt": "Return a single column diff_in_diff_estimate: (treatment post avg - treatment pre avg) - (control post avg - control pre avg).", "hint": "Build the four pre/post x treatment/control average-amount cells in a CTE, then pull each cell out with a scalar subquery and combine them with subtraction.", "solution": "WITH cells AS (SELECT g.exp_group, CASE WHEN t.txn_date<'2024-04-01' THEN 'pre' ELSE 'post' END AS period, AVG(t.amount) AS avg_amount FROM transactions t JOIN campaign g ON t.customer_id=g.customer_id GROUP BY g.exp_group, period) SELECT ROUND((SELECT avg_amount FROM cells WHERE exp_group='treatment' AND period='post') - (SELECT avg_amount FROM cells WHERE exp_group='treatment' AND period='pre') - ((SELECT avg_amount FROM cells WHERE exp_group='control' AND period='post') - (SELECT avg_amount FROM cells WHERE exp_group='control' AND period='pre')),2) AS diff_in_diff_estimate;", "ordered": false, "id": 181, "bonus": false, "clues": "<p>Build the four cells (treatment/control &times; pre/post average amount) in one CTE first, exactly like the medium causal lesson - group by exp_group and period.</p><p>To turn a specific cell into a single number, use a scalar subquery filtered down to it: <code>(SELECT avg_amount FROM cells WHERE exp_group='...' AND period='...')</code>. You'll need four of these, one per cell.</p><p>The formula itself: (treatment post &minus; treatment pre) &minus; (control post &minus; control pre). Get each of those four numbers first, then just subtract.</p>"}, {"id": 201, "topic": "Causal setup treatment and control", "diff": "recall", "ask": "Recall check: in your own words, summarize everything you've learned in this topic so far - as if you were teaching it to someone hearing about it for the very first time (the Feynman technique).", "hint": "Cover the core concepts and key terms in your own words, with at least one concrete example. There's no single right answer - explain it the way you'd say it out loud to a colleague.", "bonus": false, "recall": true, "modelAnswer": "A strong summary should explain how to identify treatment and control groups directly from a dataset - typically by joining or flagging based on an experiment-assignment table or a treatment-indicator column - and then compute an outcome metric separately for each group so they can be compared. It should mention checking that the comparison is apples-to-apples (similar group sizes/composition, a clearly defined measurement window applied consistently to both groups) and that a raw before/after difference within only the treated group, without any control group comparison, cannot establish causality on its own."}, {"id": 57, "topic": "Causal setup treatment and control", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "We ran a campaign on the first of April. Label each transaction as pre or post that date.", "prompt": "Return transaction_id, txn_date and period: label each transaction 'pre' if before 2024-04-01, otherwise 'post'.", "hint": "CASE on txn_date against the campaign date.", "solution": "SELECT transaction_id, txn_date, CASE WHEN txn_date < '2024-04-01' THEN 'pre' ELSE 'post' END AS period FROM transactions;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>A single CASE expression does the whole thing: <code>CASE WHEN txn_date &lt; '2024-04-01' THEN 'pre' ELSE 'post' END</code> - no GROUP BY or JOIN needed, this just labels every row.</p>"}, {"id": 58, "topic": "Causal setup treatment and control", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "For the campaign readout, give me average spend for treatment versus control, before versus after. That is the core diff-in-differences table.", "prompt": "Return exp_group, period and avg_amount: average transaction amount for each experiment group and pre/post period, rounded to 2 decimals. This is the diff-in-differences 2 by 2.", "hint": "Join to campaign, group by group and a pre/post CASE.", "solution": "SELECT g.exp_group, CASE WHEN t.txn_date < '2024-04-01' THEN 'pre' ELSE 'post' END AS period, ROUND(AVG(t.amount),2) AS avg_amount FROM transactions t JOIN campaign g ON t.customer_id=g.customer_id GROUP BY g.exp_group, period;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Join to campaign for the group label, CASE the date into pre/post, then GROUP BY both columns together so you get all four combinations (treatment/control &times; pre/post) as separate rows.</p>"}, {"id": 59, "topic": "Causal setup treatment and control", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Build the base table a causal model would use: per customer, their group, and their spend before and after the campaign.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, exp_group, pre_amt and post_amt: per customer, their experiment group and their total spend before and after 2024-04-01, rounded to 2 decimals. Only customers with transactions.", "hint": "Conditional sums per customer in a CTE, joined to campaign.", "solution": "WITH p AS (SELECT customer_id, SUM(CASE WHEN txn_date < '2024-04-01' THEN amount ELSE 0 END) AS pre_amt, SUM(CASE WHEN txn_date >= '2024-04-01' THEN amount ELSE 0 END) AS post_amt FROM transactions GROUP BY customer_id) SELECT c.customer_id, g.exp_group, ROUND(p.pre_amt,2) AS pre_amt, ROUND(p.post_amt,2) AS post_amt FROM customers c JOIN campaign g ON c.customer_id=g.customer_id JOIN p ON p.customer_id=c.customer_id;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Build conditional sums per customer in a CTE - <code>SUM(CASE WHEN txn_date &lt; '2024-04-01' THEN amount ELSE 0 END)</code> for pre, and the mirror-image condition for post, both in the same GROUP BY customer_id.</p><p>Join that CTE to both customers and campaign in the outer query to attach exp_group.</p>"}, {"id": 136, "topic": "Causal setup treatment and control", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Before any analysis, sanity-check the split: how many customers landed in each experiment group?", "prompt": "Return exp_group and customer_count: the number of customers in each experiment group.", "hint": "A simple GROUP BY on the campaign table.", "solution": "SELECT exp_group, COUNT(*) AS customer_count FROM campaign GROUP BY exp_group;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>No JOIN needed at all - <code>campaign</code> already has exp_group, just GROUP BY it and COUNT(*).</p>"}, {"id": 137, "topic": "Causal setup treatment and control", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Alongside the average-amount 2x2, the team also wants transaction VOLUME per group and period.", "prompt": "Return exp_group, period and txn_count: the number of transactions per experiment group and pre/post period.", "hint": "Same group-and-period structure as the average-amount version, just COUNT(*) instead.", "solution": "SELECT g.exp_group, CASE WHEN t.txn_date < '2024-04-01' THEN 'pre' ELSE 'post' END AS period, COUNT(*) AS txn_count FROM transactions t JOIN campaign g ON t.customer_id=g.customer_id GROUP BY g.exp_group, period;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Identical structure to the average-amount 2x2 (join to campaign, CASE the period, GROUP BY group and period) - just swap <code>AVG(amount)</code> for <code>COUNT(*)</code>.</p>"}, {"id": 138, "topic": "Causal setup treatment and control", "diff": "medium", "ordered": false, "ask": "Before modelling, the team wants each customer's all-time total spend tagged with their experiment group.", "prompt": "Return customer_id, exp_group and total_amount: each customer's total spend and experiment group.", "hint": "Join transactions to campaign, group by customer and group.", "solution": "SELECT t.customer_id, g.exp_group, ROUND(SUM(t.amount),2) AS total_amount FROM transactions t JOIN campaign g ON t.customer_id=g.customer_id GROUP BY t.customer_id, g.exp_group;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Join transactions to campaign, GROUP BY customer_id and exp_group together, SUM(amount) - no date logic needed here, this is an all-time total, not a pre/post breakdown.</p>"}, {"id": 139, "topic": "Causal setup treatment and control", "diff": "hard", "ordered": false, "ask": "Go one step further than the 2x2 table: compute the actual difference-in-differences estimate as a single number.", "prompt": "Return diff_in_diff: (treatment post-avg minus treatment pre-avg) minus (control post-avg minus control pre-avg), rounded to 2 decimals.", "hint": "Build the 2x2 averages in a CTE, then pull each cell out with a CASE inside MAX() to do the arithmetic.", "solution": "WITH cell AS (SELECT g.exp_group, CASE WHEN t.txn_date < '2024-04-01' THEN 'pre' ELSE 'post' END AS period, AVG(t.amount) AS avg_amount FROM transactions t JOIN campaign g ON t.customer_id=g.customer_id GROUP BY g.exp_group, period) SELECT ROUND((MAX(CASE WHEN exp_group='treatment' AND period='post' THEN avg_amount END) - MAX(CASE WHEN exp_group='treatment' AND period='pre' THEN avg_amount END)) - (MAX(CASE WHEN exp_group='control' AND period='post' THEN avg_amount END) - MAX(CASE WHEN exp_group='control' AND period='pre' THEN avg_amount END)),2) AS diff_in_diff FROM cell;", "bonus": true, "clues": "<p>Build the four pre/post &times; treatment/control average-amount cells in a CTE first, exactly like the 2x2 table.</p><p>To pull a specific cell out as a single number in the outer query, <code>MAX(CASE WHEN exp_group='...' AND period='...' THEN avg_amount END)</code> works well since only one row in the CTE matches each condition.</p><p>The formula: (treatment post &minus; treatment pre) &minus; (control post &minus; control pre).</p>"}]

let current=0;
const solved=new Set();
const flagged=new Set();
const savedQueries={};

function topicIndices(topic){const out=[];QUESTIONS.forEach((q,i)=>{if(q.topic===topic)out.push(i);});return out;}
function topicRequiredIndices(topic){return topicIndices(topic).filter(i=>!QUESTIONS[i].bonus&&!QUESTIONS[i].recall);}
function topicDone(topic){return topicRequiredIndices(topic).filter(i=>solved.has(QUESTIONS[i].id)).length;}
function requiredCount(){return QUESTIONS.filter(q=>!q.bonus&&!q.recall).length;}
function requiredSolvedCount(){let n=0;QUESTIONS.forEach(q=>{if(!q.bonus&&!q.recall&&solved.has(q.id))n++;});return n;}

function buildSidebar(){
  let html="";
  TOPICS.forEach((topic,ti)=>{
    const idxs=topicIndices(topic);
    const reqLen=topicRequiredIndices(topic).length;
    html+='<div class="topic" data-topic="'+ti+'">';
    html+='<div class="topic-head"><span class="tname">'+(ti+1)+'. '+topic+'</span><span class="frac" id="frac-'+ti+'">'+topicDone(topic)+'/'+reqLen+'</span><span class="topic-chev">hide</span></div>';
    html+='<div class="tbar"><i id="tbar-'+ti+'" style="width:'+(topicDone(topic)/reqLen*100)+'%"></i></div><div class="pad">';
    idxs.forEach(i=>{
      const q=QUESTIONS[i];
      const label=q.recall?"Recall: summarize this topic":(q.prompt.slice(0,40)+(q.prompt.length>40?"...":""));
      html+='<button class="qbtn'+(solved.has(q.id)?" done":"")+(q.bonus?" bonus":"")+(q.recall?" recall":"")+'" data-i="'+i+'"><span class="dot">'+(solved.has(q.id)?"\u2713":"")+'</span><span class="star">'+(flagged.has(q.id)?"\u2605":"")+'</span><span class="lbl">'+label+'</span></button>';
    });
    html+='</div></div>';
  });
  return html;
}
function buildMain(){
  return '<main>'
    +'<div class="qhead" id="qhead"></div>'
    +'<div class="readystrip"><div class="rt" id="rtTitle"></div><span class="rbar"><i id="rbar"></i></span><span class="rlabel" id="rlabel"></span></div>'
    +'<div class="lesson" id="lesson"><div class="lesson-head" id="lessonHead"><span class="lh" id="lessonTitle">Lesson</span><span class="chev" id="chev">hide</span></div><div class="lesson-body" id="lessonBody"></div></div>'
    +'<div class="ask" id="ask"><span class="albl">How it comes up in the interview</span><span class="atext" id="askText"></span></div>'
    +'<div class="promptbox collapsed" id="promptBox"><div class="prompt-head" id="promptHead"><span class="plbl">Write the query</span><span class="chev" id="promptChev">show</span></div><p class="prompt" id="prompt"><span id="promptText"></span></p><p class="hint" id="hint"></p></div><div class="cluesbox collapsed" id="cluesBox" style="display:none"><div class="prompt-head" id="cluesHead"><span class="plbl">Need a nudge?</span><span class="chev" id="cluesChev">show</span></div><div class="clues-body" id="cluesBody"></div></div>'
    +'<div class="editor-wrap">'
      +'<div class="editor-bar" id="pycalcBar"><span id="editorLabel">query.sql</span><button class="schema-toggle" id="schemaBtn">view schema</button></div>'
      +'<textarea id="sql" spellcheck="false" autocapitalize="off" autocomplete="off"></textarea>'
      +'<textarea id="writtenAnswer" style="display:none" spellcheck="true" placeholder="Type your answer here - a few sentences covering the key ideas is plenty. Talk through it the way you would out loud in the interview."></textarea>'
    +'</div>'
    +'<div class="actions"><button class="act run" id="runBtn">Run and check</button><button class="act run" id="checkAnswerBtn" style="display:none">Check answer</button><button class="act" id="solBtn">Show solution</button><button class="act" id="explainBtn">Explain solution</button><button class="act" id="explainWrongBtn" style="display:none">Explain my error</button><select id="explainModel" class="explain-model"><option value="sonnet">Sonnet 5</option><option value="haiku">Haiku 4.5</option></select><button class="act" id="resetBtn">Clear</button><button class="act" id="prevBtn">&larr; Previous</button><button class="act" id="nextBtn">Next &rarr;</button><span class="kbd">Cmd / Ctrl + Enter &middot; Shift + &larr;/&rarr;</span></div>'
    +'<div class="verdict" id="verdict"><span class="ic" id="vic"></span><span id="vmsg"></span></div>'
    +'<div class="results" id="resultsBox" style="display:none"><h3>Your result</h3><div class="tablescroll" id="tableScroll"></div><div class="rowcount" id="rowCount"></div></div>'
    +'<div class="solution" id="solution"><div class="lbl" id="solutionLbl">One correct solution (there are often several)</div><pre id="solText"></pre><div id="solProse" style="display:none"></div></div>'
    +'<div class="explainbox" id="explainBox" style="display:none"><div class="lbl">Claude explains</div><div id="explainText"></div></div>'
    +'<div class="schema" id="schema"></div></main>';
}
function renderTable(res){
  if(!res.columns.length) return '<div style="padding:14px;color:var(--muted);font-family:var(--mono);font-size:12.5px">No columns returned.</div>';
  let h='<table><thead><tr>';res.columns.forEach(c=>h+='<th>'+c+'</th>');h+='</tr></thead><tbody>';
  res.values.slice(0,200).forEach(row=>{h+='<tr>';row.forEach(v=>{h+=(v===null||v===undefined)?'<td class="null">NULL</td>':'<td>'+String(v)+'</td>';});h+='</tr>';});
  return h+'</tbody></table>';
}

function updateTopic(topic){
  const ti=TOPICS.indexOf(topic);const reqLen=topicRequiredIndices(topic).length;const done=topicDone(topic);
  const frac=document.getElementById("frac-"+ti);
  frac.textContent=done+"/"+reqLen;frac.className="frac"+(done===reqLen?" full":"");
  document.getElementById("tbar-"+ti).style.width=(done/reqLen*100)+"%";
  if(document.getElementById("rtTitle") && QUESTIONS[current].topic===topic){
    setReadystrip(topic,done,reqLen);
  }
}
function setReadystrip(topic,done,total){
  document.getElementById("rtTitle").innerHTML=topic+"<small>Topic "+(TOPICS.indexOf(topic)+1)+" of "+TOPICS.length+"</small>";
  document.getElementById("rbar").style.width=(done/total*100)+"%";
  const lab=document.getElementById("rlabel");
  if(done===0){lab.textContent="Not started";lab.className="rlabel";}
  else if(done<total){lab.textContent="In progress "+done+"/"+total;lab.className="rlabel prep";}
  else{lab.textContent="Ready \u2713";lab.className="rlabel ready";}
}
function updateProgress(){
  const done=requiredSolvedCount(),total=requiredCount();
  document.getElementById("doneCount").textContent=done;
  document.getElementById("progBar").style.width=Math.round(done/total*100)+"%";
  updateQuizGate(done,total);
}
function updateQuizGate(done,total){
  const btn=document.getElementById("quizBtn");
  if(!btn) return;
  if(done>=total){btn.classList.remove("locked");btn.removeAttribute("aria-disabled");}
  else{btn.classList.add("locked");btn.setAttribute("aria-disabled","true");}
}

function loadQuestion(i){
  current=i;const q=QUESTIONS[i];
  document.getElementById("qtick").textContent="Q"+(i+1);
  document.getElementById("qhead").innerHTML='<button class="star-toggle" id="starToggle" type="button" aria-label="Mark to come back to">☆</button><span class="pill '+q.diff+'">'+q.diff+'</span><span class="pill">'+q.topic+'</span><span class="pill est" id="estBadge">'+estimateLabel(q.diff)+'</span><span class="qtimer" id="qtimerMain">00:00</span><span class="cleared '+(solved.has(q.id)?"show":"")+'" id="clearedStamp">cleared</span>';
  renderStar(q.id);
  document.getElementById("starToggle").addEventListener("click",()=>toggleFlag(q.id));
  startQuestionTimer(q.diff);
  const lessonCard=document.getElementById("lesson");
  if(q.recall){
    lessonCard.style.display="none";
  }else{
    lessonCard.style.display="";
    const lessonContent=(typeof LESSONS[q.topic]==="object")?LESSONS[q.topic][q.subtopic]:LESSONS[q.topic];
    document.getElementById("lessonBody").innerHTML=lessonContent;
  }
  document.getElementById("askText").textContent=q.ask;
  document.getElementById("promptText").textContent=q.prompt||"";
  document.getElementById("promptBox").classList.add("collapsed");
  document.getElementById("promptChev").textContent="show";
  document.getElementById("hint").innerHTML="<b>Hint:</b> "+q.hint;
  const cluesBox=document.getElementById("cluesBox");
  if(q.clues){
    cluesBox.style.display="block";
    cluesBox.classList.add("collapsed");
    document.getElementById("cluesChev").textContent="show";
    document.getElementById("cluesBody").innerHTML=q.clues;
  }else{
    cluesBox.style.display="none";
  }
  document.getElementById("pycalcBar").style.display=q.recall?"none":"flex";
  document.getElementById("sql").style.display=q.recall?"none":"block";
  document.getElementById("writtenAnswer").style.display=q.recall?"block":"none";
  document.getElementById("runBtn").style.display=q.recall?"none":"";
  document.getElementById("checkAnswerBtn").style.display=q.recall?"":"none";
  document.getElementById("schemaBtn").style.display=q.recall?"none":"";
  if(q.recall) document.getElementById("writtenAnswer").value=savedQueries[q.id]||"";
  else document.getElementById("sql").value=savedQueries[q.id]||"";
  document.getElementById("verdict").className="verdict";
  document.getElementById("resultsBox").style.display="none";
  document.getElementById("solution").className="solution";
  document.getElementById("explainBox").style.display="none";
  setExplainWrongVisible(false);
  const solLbl=document.getElementById("solutionLbl"),solText=document.getElementById("solText"),solProse=document.getElementById("solProse");
  if(q.recall){
    solLbl.textContent="What a strong summary should cover";
    solText.style.display="none";
    solProse.style.display="block";solProse.innerHTML=renderMarkdown(q.modelAnswer);
  }else{
    solLbl.textContent="One correct solution (there are often several)";
    solText.style.display="block";solText.textContent=formatSQL(q.solution);
    solProse.style.display="none";
  }
  const reqLen=topicRequiredIndices(q.topic).length;setReadystrip(q.topic,topicDone(q.topic),reqLen);
  document.querySelectorAll(".qbtn").forEach(b=>b.classList.toggle("active",+b.dataset.i===i));
  document.getElementById("prevBtn").disabled=(i===0);
  document.getElementById("nextBtn").disabled=(i===QUESTIONS.length-1);
  document.querySelector("main").scrollIntoView({behavior:"smooth",block:"start"});
  if(q.recall) document.getElementById("writtenAnswer").focus({preventScroll:true});
  else document.getElementById("sql").focus({preventScroll:true});
}
function goToQuestion(delta){
  const next=current+delta;
  if(next>=0 && next<QUESTIONS.length) loadQuestion(next);
}
function markSolved(i){
  const id=QUESTIONS[i].id;
  if(!solved.has(id)){
    solved.add(id);
    const btn=document.querySelector('.qbtn[data-i="'+i+'"]');
    if(btn){btn.classList.add("done");btn.querySelector(".dot").textContent="\u2713";}
    const st=document.getElementById("clearedStamp");if(st)st.classList.add("show");
    updateProgress();updateTopic(QUESTIONS[i].topic);
  }
}
async function doCheckWritten(){
  const q=QUESTIONS[current];
  const answer=document.getElementById("writtenAnswer").value.trim();
  if(!answer) return;
  savedQueries[q.id]=answer;
  const btn=document.getElementById("checkAnswerBtn");
  const orig=btn.textContent;
  btn.disabled=true;btn.textContent="Checking...";
  const box=document.getElementById("explainBox"),txt=document.getElementById("explainText");
  box.style.display="block";
  txt.innerHTML='<div class="explain-loading"><span class="spinner"></span>Reading through your answer\u2026</div>';
  const verdict=document.getElementById("verdict"),vic=document.getElementById("vic"),vmsg=document.getElementById("vmsg");
  try{
    const payload={track:"sql",mode:"trainer",questionId:q.id,kind:"grade",ask:q.ask,solution:q.modelAnswer,studentAnswer:answer,model:getExplainModel()};
    const r=await requestExplanation(payload);
    txt.innerHTML=renderMarkdown(r.content);
    box.scrollIntoView({behavior:"smooth",block:"nearest"});
    const strong=/\*\*strong\*\*/i.test(r.content) || /^strong/i.test(r.content.replace(/[*_]/g,""));
    verdict.className="verdict "+(strong?"ok":"no");
    vic.textContent=strong?"\u2713":"~";
    vmsg.textContent=strong?"Graded as strong - see feedback below.":"See feedback below for what to add.";
    logAttempt(q.id,answer,strong);
    if(strong) markSolved(current);
  }catch(e){
    txt.textContent=e.message;
  }finally{
    btn.disabled=false;btn.textContent=orig;
  }
}
function doRunSql(){
  const q=QUESTIONS[current];const sql=document.getElementById("sql").value.trim();
  const verdict=document.getElementById("verdict"),vic=document.getElementById("vic"),vmsg=document.getElementById("vmsg"),resultsBox=document.getElementById("resultsBox");
  if(!sql)return;
  let userRes;
  try{userRes=runSQL(sql);}catch(err){verdict.className="verdict no";vic.textContent="!";vmsg.textContent="SQL error: "+err.message;resultsBox.style.display="none";setExplainWrongVisible(true);return;}
  resultsBox.style.display="block";
  document.getElementById("tableScroll").innerHTML=renderTable(userRes);
  const n=userRes.values.length;
  document.getElementById("rowCount").textContent=n+" row"+(n===1?"":"s")+" returned"+(n>200?" (showing first 200)":"");
  let solRes;try{solRes=runSQL(q.solution);}catch(e){solRes={columns:[],values:[]};}
  const g=grade(userRes,solRes,q.ordered);
  if(g.ok){verdict.className="verdict ok";vic.textContent="\u2713";vmsg.textContent=g.msg;markSolved(current);savedQueries[q.id]=sql;}
  else{verdict.className="verdict no";vic.textContent="\u2717";vmsg.textContent=g.msg;}
  logAttempt(q.id,sql,g.ok);
  setExplainWrongVisible(!g.ok);
}
function doRun(){
  const q=QUESTIONS[current];
  if(q.recall) return doCheckWritten();
  return doRunSql();
}

let supabaseClient=null, currentUser=null, startIndex=0;

function initSupabase(){
  supabaseClient=window.supabase.createClient(window.__SUPABASE_URL__, window.__SUPABASE_ANON_KEY__);
}

function showSignOutBtn(){
  const btn=document.getElementById("signOutBtn");
  btn.classList.add("visible");
  btn.addEventListener("click",()=>{
    supabaseClient.auth.signOut().then(()=>window.location.reload());
  });
}

function requireAuth(){
  return supabaseClient.auth.getSession().then(({data:{session}})=>{
    if(session){currentUser=session.user;showSignOutBtn();return;}
    const gate=document.getElementById("authGate");
    gate.style.display="flex";
    return new Promise(resolve=>{
      document.getElementById("googleSignInBtn").addEventListener("click",()=>{
        supabaseClient.auth.signInWithOAuth({provider:"google",options:{redirectTo:window.location.origin}});
      });
      supabaseClient.auth.onAuthStateChange((event,session)=>{
        if(session && !currentUser){
          currentUser=session.user;
          gate.style.display="none";
          showSignOutBtn();
          resolve();
        }
      });
    });
  });
}

function hydrateProgress(){
  return supabaseClient.from("attempts").select("question_id,passed,query_text,created_at").eq("mode","trainer").eq("track","sql").order("created_at",{ascending:true})
    .then(({data,error})=>{
      if(error||!data) return;
      data.forEach(r=>{
        const q=QUESTIONS.find(x=>x.id===r.question_id);
        if(r.passed)solved.add(r.question_id);
        if(r.passed||(q&&q.recall))savedQueries[r.question_id]=r.query_text;
      });
      if(data.length) startIndex=data[data.length-1].question_id;
    });
}

function hydrateFlags(){
  return fetchFlaggedIds("sql","trainer").then(ids=>ids.forEach(id=>flagged.add(id)));
}

function toggleFlag(id){
  const on=!flagged.has(id);
  if(on) flagged.add(id); else flagged.delete(id);
  setQuestionFlag("sql","trainer",id,on);
  renderStar(id);
  const btn=document.querySelector('.qbtn[data-i="'+current+'"]');
  if(btn){
    const st=btn.querySelector(".star");
    if(st) st.textContent=on?"★":"";
  }
}

function renderStar(id){
  const btn=document.getElementById("starToggle");
  if(!btn) return;
  const on=flagged.has(id);
  btn.textContent=on?"★":"☆";
  btn.classList.toggle("on",on);
  btn.setAttribute("aria-label",on?"Marked to come back to":"Mark to come back to");
}

async function runExplain(kind){
  const q=QUESTIONS[current];
  const btn=document.getElementById(kind==="solution"?"explainBtn":"explainWrongBtn");
  const box=document.getElementById("explainBox");
  const txt=document.getElementById("explainText");
  const orig=btn.textContent;
  btn.disabled=true;btn.textContent="Thinking...";
  box.style.display="block";
  txt.innerHTML='<div class="explain-loading"><span class="spinner"></span>Thinking through the solution…</div>';
  try{
    const payload={track:"sql",mode:"trainer",questionId:q.id,kind,ask:q.ask,prompt:q.prompt||"",solution:q.recall?q.modelAnswer:q.solution,model:getExplainModel()};
    if(kind==="attempt")payload.userCode=q.recall?(savedQueries[q.id]||""):document.getElementById("sql").value;
    const r=await requestExplanation(payload);
    txt.innerHTML=renderMarkdown(r.content);
    box.scrollIntoView({behavior:"smooth",block:"nearest"});
  }catch(e){
    txt.textContent=e.message;
  }finally{
    btn.disabled=false;btn.textContent=orig;
  }
}

function logAttempt(questionId,queryText,passed){
  if(!supabaseClient||!currentUser) return;
  supabaseClient.from("attempts").insert({question_id:questionId,query_text:queryText,passed,mode:"trainer",track:"sql"}).then(()=>{});
}

initSupabase();
initThemeToggle();
initTrainerSelect();

window.addEventListener("scroll",()=>{
  document.body.classList.toggle("scrolled",window.scrollY>30);
  syncHeaderHeight();
  setTimeout(syncHeaderHeight,200);
},{passive:true});

if(window.visualViewport){
  window.visualViewport.addEventListener("resize",()=>{
    document.body.classList.toggle("scrolled",window.scrollY>30);
    syncHeaderHeight();
  });
}

window.addEventListener("resize",syncHeaderHeight);
syncHeaderHeight();
setTimeout(syncHeaderHeight,300);

Promise.all([
  initSqlJs({locateFile:f=>"https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/sql.js/1.8.0/"+f}),
  requireAuth().then(()=>Promise.all([hydrateProgress(),hydrateFlags()]))
])
.then(([SQL])=>{
  db=new SQL.Database();db.run(SCHEMA_SQL);
  const app=document.getElementById("app");app.className="";
  app.innerHTML='<div class="wrap"><aside id="sidebar">'+buildSidebar()+'</aside>'+buildMain()+'<div class="col-resizer" id="colResizer"></div><div class="right-col" id="rightCol"></div></div>';
  document.getElementById("schema").innerHTML=buildSchema();
  document.getElementById("totalCount").textContent=requiredCount();
  document.querySelectorAll(".qbtn").forEach(b=>b.addEventListener("click",()=>loadQuestion(+b.dataset.i)));
  document.getElementById("runBtn").addEventListener("click",doRun);
  document.getElementById("checkAnswerBtn").addEventListener("click",doRun);
  document.getElementById("explainBtn").addEventListener("click",()=>runExplain("solution"));
  document.getElementById("explainWrongBtn").addEventListener("click",()=>runExplain("attempt"));
  initExplainModelSelect();
  initShellLayout();
  initTopicCollapse("collapsedTopicsSql");
  document.getElementById("solBtn").addEventListener("click",()=>document.getElementById("solution").classList.toggle("show"));
  document.getElementById("resetBtn").addEventListener("click",()=>{
    const q=QUESTIONS[current];
    if(q.recall){document.getElementById("writtenAnswer").value="";document.getElementById("writtenAnswer").focus();}
    else{document.getElementById("sql").value="";document.getElementById("sql").focus();}
  });
  document.getElementById("schemaBtn").addEventListener("click",()=>document.getElementById("schema").scrollIntoView({behavior:"smooth"}));
  document.getElementById("prevBtn").addEventListener("click",()=>goToQuestion(-1));
  document.getElementById("nextBtn").addEventListener("click",()=>goToQuestion(1));
  const lh=document.getElementById("lessonHead");
  lh.addEventListener("click",()=>{const l=document.getElementById("lesson");l.classList.toggle("collapsed");document.getElementById("chev").textContent=l.classList.contains("collapsed")?"show":"hide";});
  const ph=document.getElementById("promptHead");
  ph.addEventListener("click",()=>{const p=document.getElementById("promptBox");p.classList.toggle("collapsed");document.getElementById("promptChev").textContent=p.classList.contains("collapsed")?"show":"hide";});
  const ch=document.getElementById("cluesHead");
  ch.addEventListener("click",()=>{const c=document.getElementById("cluesBox");c.classList.toggle("collapsed");document.getElementById("cluesChev").textContent=c.classList.contains("collapsed")?"show":"hide";});
  const ta=document.getElementById("sql");
  ta.addEventListener("keydown",e=>{
    if((e.metaKey||e.ctrlKey)&&e.key==="Enter"){e.preventDefault();doRun();}
    if(e.key==="Tab"){e.preventDefault();const s=ta.selectionStart,en=ta.selectionEnd;ta.value=ta.value.slice(0,s)+"  "+ta.value.slice(en);ta.selectionStart=ta.selectionEnd=s+2;}
  });
  const wa=document.getElementById("writtenAnswer");
  wa.addEventListener("keydown",e=>{
    if((e.metaKey||e.ctrlKey)&&e.key==="Enter"){e.preventDefault();doRun();}
  });
  document.addEventListener("keydown",e=>{
    if(!e.shiftKey || document.activeElement===ta || document.activeElement===wa) return;
    if(e.key==="ArrowLeft"){e.preventDefault();goToQuestion(-1);}
    if(e.key==="ArrowRight"){e.preventDefault();goToQuestion(1);}
  });
  updateProgress();loadQuestion(startIndex);
})
.catch(err=>{document.getElementById("app").innerHTML='<div class="loading">Could not load the SQL engine. Check your connection and reload. ('+err.message+')</div>';});
